{"level":30,"time":1776954217954,"pid":39427,"hostname":"Williams-MacBook-Pro.local","step":"review-runs","item":"1.md","index":3,"runIndex":"run-1","type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_01UEJ3uDRpPH7JoiyNEo2L1n","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"This is a comprehensive grouping about overhead and vertical electrical clearances. The grouping covers:\n\n1. Permanent clearances from overhead distribution lines (7.5 ft horizontal, 15 ft radius)\n2. Service drop clearances\n3. Underground facility separations\n4. Transmission line requirements\n5. SCM staging areas and maintenance access drives\n6. And various specific clearance requirements\n\nLet me now understand the project structure and read the relevant site plan documents. First, I should check the project README to understand what documents are available.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":7,"cache_creation_input_tokens":9108,"cache_read_input_tokens":23751,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":9108},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"51587e83-ba72-4647-83cb-6d408affd332","uuid":"4fa24e36-6c18-48a6-8238-35672faff93c"}
{"level":30,"time":1776954217967,"pid":39427,"hostname":"Williams-MacBook-Pro.local","step":"review-runs","item":"1.md","index":9,"runIndex":"run-3","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01AribJVXHHy6kTYJzYEHAqs","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# Overhead and Vertical Electrical Clearances\n     2→\n     3→## Description\n     4→\n     5→This grouping covers minimum clearance requirements between structures, utilities, and energized overhead electrical conductors, as well as minimum separations between utilities at crossings and in parallel routing. It also encompasses vertical clearance requirements for staging areas, maintenance access drives, and work zone operations during construction. Reviewers apply these checks whenever overhead utilities remain on or adjacent to a site, utility crossings are proposed, or construction activities occur near electrical infrastructure.\n     6→\n     7→## Regulatory Overview\n     8→\n     9→**Permanent Clearances from AE Overhead Distribution Lines (UCM 1.10.3)**\n    10→\n    11→Customer facilities — defined broadly as any customer-side structure, improvement, or installation, including buildings, parking garages, light poles, signs, billboards, chimneys, antennas, tanks, and vaults — must maintain two simultaneous and independent clearances from AE overhead distribution conductors: (1) a **7-foot 6-inch horizontal sky-to-ground clearance** measured from the outermost (outside) conductor, and (2) a **15-foot-radius clearance** from overhead distribution primary and neutral conductors. No customer facility may be installed under or over AE overhead distribution facilities — this prohibition is absolute and separate from the numerical clearance requirements. Where a vault is proposed near overhead power lines, the applicant must provide AE with a survey showing proximity to AE primary voltage facilities, and Austin Energy written clearance approval must be obtained and included in the project file. Both clearance thresholds must be satisfied simultaneously; an installation that clears 7.5 feet horizontally but falls within the 15-foot radial zone still violates UCM 1.10.3.\n    12→\n    13→The Land Development Code separately requires an **overhead utility zone** — a 10-foot-radius buffer from the energized conductor — to be graphically delineated on site plans when overhead utilities remain on the site. No building or structure may encroach into this zone. This LDC requirement applies **only when the site fronts a Core Transit Corridor or Urban Roadway** (LDC Subchapter E, § 2.2.2.B.3 for CTCs; extended to Urban Roadways by LDC Subchapter E, § 2.2.3.B.3). **Core Transit Corridors** are 20 enumerated street segments: South First St (north of Ben White), E 7th St (west of Pleasant Valley), E/W 5th St (various limits), E/W 6th St (various limits), W 35th/38th St corridor, Airport Blvd (Lamar to I-35), Anderson Ln (Burnet to Mopac), Barton Springs Rd (east of Robert E. Lee), Burnet Rd (45th to Anderson), South Congress Ave (north of Stassney), Guadalupe St, Lamar Blvd (Banyon to Ben White), MLK Jr. Blvd (Pearl to Airport), Riverside Dr (Lamar to E. Ben White/Hwy 71), Cameron Rd (51st to US-290), 51st St (Cameron to Manor), Gaston Place, and Briarcliff Blvd. **Urban Roadways** are roads within a boundary polygon roughly bounded by 183/Hwy 71/Loop 1/Lake Austin/Exposition/35th St/Loop 1/RM 2222/Mesa/Spicewood Springs/360/Great Hills Trail/183/Braker/Burnet, excluding CTCs and Highways. The UCM 1.10.3 clearances (7.5-ft horizontal and 15-ft radius) apply universally to all customer facilities regardless of street type; the LDC 10-ft zone is an additional, street-type-dependent requirement.\n    14→\n    15→**Critical measurement rule:** All clearances must be measured from the **outermost (outside) conductor**, not from the utility pole centerline. On three-phase configurations, the outside conductor may be several feet offset from the pole center. Plans showing clearance dimensions anchored to the pole symbol rather than the conductor are non-compliant even when stated distances appear adequate. This is the single most common measurement error on AE clearance exhibits.\n    16→\n    17→**Service-Drop Clearances and Customer Service Entrance Facilities (UCM 1.10.6.1 and 1.10.6.2)**\n    18→\n    19→Service-drop conductors must maintain minimum vertical clearance from final grade based on the area type: **12 ft** over pedestrian-only residential areas; **16 ft** over residential driveways, or parking lots and alleys not subject to truck traffic, and commercial services over pedestrian areas; **18 ft** over roads, streets, alleys, parking lots, and any other area subject to truck traffic. An area is \"subject to truck traffic\" when commercial freight, delivery, refuse trucks, or other heavy commercial vehicles are reasonably expected based on land use. Public streets always require 18 ft. Alleys and parking lots may require either 16-ft or 18-ft depending on use — when ambiguous, apply 18-ft as the conservative default. Customer service entrance facilities (service equipment, service conductors, one-point rack) must also maintain a **10-foot minimum horizontal clearance** from AE poles and overhead lines other than the service drop (UCM 1.10.6.1).\n    20→\n    21→**AE Underground Facility Separations and Utility Easement Routing (UCM 1.10.5.A and UCM 2.9.1.A.3)**\n    22→\n    23→AE underground facilities (cable, conduit, duct structure, pull-boxes) require a **12-inch minimum clearance** — both horizontally and vertically — from other utilities at **perpendicular crossings only**; fuel lines and high-pressure gas (≥60 psi) require 36-inch minimum separation. This rule governs perpendicular crossings only; parallel routing of other utilities alongside AE underground conduit requires a separate written joint-trench agreement from AE. Non-electrical utilities may cross a public utility easement (PUE) or electrical easement only perpendicularly or up to 45° from perpendicular — **parallel routing within easement boundaries is prohibited at all times** (UCM 2.9.1.A.3). Vertical separation standards for crossings with AW water/wastewater mains are: 12-inch minimum when AW main is above; 18-inch minimum when AW main is below (UCM 2.9.1.B.3). For trenchless crossings under water lines, the minimum clearance is the greater of (a) two times the excavation diameter/span or (b) 36 inches (UCM 2.9.2.B.17). UCM 2.9.2.B.19 governs bedding and backfill material (CLSM) for open-cut crossings under water mains — it is a material specification, not an additional vertical clearance standard; the separation standard for open-cut crossings is in UCM 2.9.1.B.3.\n    24→\n    25→**Transmission Line Requirements (UCM 1.14 series)**\n    26→\n    27→Transmission lines are electric facilities at **60 kV or higher** (UCM §1.17.0 Glossary). Austin Energy operates transmission at 69 kV, 138 kV, and 345 kV. Physical indicators: substantially taller poles or lattice towers, long insulators (≥3 ft), conductors spread widely apart, shield wire at top. Transmission clearance envelopes are complex, site-specific, three-dimensional shapes that cannot be reduced to a table; Austin Energy engineering must evaluate specific locations (UCM 1.14.3.1). OSHA working clearances during construction, by voltage: **10 ft for 69 kV; 11.33 ft for 138 kV; 15.33 ft for 345 kV** (UCM 1.14.3.2). Transmission circuits cannot be de-energized for nearby construction, even temporarily. Key construction requirements (UCM 1.14.9): pre-construction safety meeting with AE ≥48 hours before commencement; barricades erected 10 ft from all transmission structures (including guys and anchors) within construction area; warning signs under overhead facilities; no materials storage or spoils within transmission easement; tower cranes and temporary structures positioned so they could not fall within 20 ft of transmission facility. The transmission ROW access corridor must be maintained at 25 ft wide with **16-ft minimum vertical clearance** along the full ROW length (UCM 1.14.4).\n    28→\n    29→**SCM Staging Areas and Maintenance Access Drives (DCM 1.2.4.E)**\n    30→\n    31→Permanent SCM staging areas (required for COA-maintained SCMs and residential single-family/duplex development SCMs) must maintain **14-ft minimum vertical clearance** from existing and proposed vegetation and all other objects (DCM 1.2.4.E.2.e). Maintenance access drives (required for all residential development SCMs, all COA-maintained SCMs, and commercial SCMs where access from ROW or grade exceeds 20%) must maintain the same **14-ft minimum vertical clearance** (DCM 1.2.4.E.1.e.ii). Both requirements include vegetation heights at maturity. Neither requirement applies if combined total SCM storage volume ≤5,000 cu ft AND maximum ponding depth ≤3 ft.\n    32→\n    33→**Key Thresholds:**\n    34→- 10 ft: Overhead utility zone radius from energized conductor (LDC Subchapter E, § 2.2.2.B.3 — CTC/Urban Roadway only)\n    35→- 7.5 ft: Horizontal sky-to-ground clearance from AE overhead conductors for all customer facilities (UCM 1.10.3)\n    36→- 15 ft: Radial clearance from AE overhead distribution primary and neutral conductors (UCM 1.10.3)\n    37→- 10 ft: Minimum horizontal clearance from AE poles for customer service entrance facilities (UCM 1.10.6.1); OSHA working clearance from lines ≤50 kV (UCM 1.10.2)\n    38→- 18 ft: Service-drop clearance over truck traffic areas (UCM 1.10.6.2)\n    39→- 16 ft: Service-drop clearance over residential driveways / non-truck alleys/lots (UCM 1.10.6.2); transmission ROW access corridor vertical clearance (UCM 1.14.4)\n    40→- 12 in: AE underground facility separation from other utilities at perpendicular crossings (UCM 1.10.5.A)\n    41→- 18 in / 12 in: Water/wastewater main crossing separation — AW main above / AW main below (UCM 2.9.1.B.3)\n    42→- 36 in (or 2× excavation diameter): Trenchless crossing clearance from water line (UCM 2.9.2.B.17)\n    43→- 14 ft: SCM staging area and maintenance access drive vertical clearance (DCM 1.2.4.E.2.e; DCM 1.2.4.E.1.e.ii)\n    44→- 24 in: ROW facility proximity requiring AULCC ROW assignment change approval (§ 14-11-180(C))\n    45→\n    46→**Exceptions:**\n    47→- SCM staging area and maintenance access drive clearance requirements exempt when combined pond volume ≤5,000 cu ft AND ponding depth ≤3 ft\n    48→- AW may approve reduced crossing vertical separations under constrained conditions (6-in vs. 12-in; 12-in vs. 18-in)\n    49→- LDC overhead utility zone (10-ft radius) applies only on Core Transit Corridors and Urban Roadways; UCM 1.10.3 permanent clearances apply universally regardless of street type\n    50→\n    51→**Common Violation Patterns:**\n    52→- Clearances dimensioned from utility pole centerline rather than from the outermost conductor\n    53→- Plan states \"vault approved by AE Design\" without including the approval letter in the project file\n    54→- Proposed utility within 24 inches of existing ROW facility with no AULCC approval or deviation documentation\n    55→- Non-electrical utilities running parallel within an electrical or PUE easement boundary\n    56→- Maintenance access drive shown with tree canopy overhead where canopy at maturity would reduce clearance below 14 ft, or no clearance labeled\n    57→- AE underground conduit in joint trench with other utilities without written AE agreement\n    58→\n    59→## Code References\n    60→\n    61→- UCM 1.10.2 — Working clearances from energized overhead electric utility lines; incorporates OSHA 1910.333 (10-ft from lines ≤50 kV for unqualified persons) and Texas H&S Code § 752.004 requirements by reference\n    62→- UCM 1.10.3 — Permanent clearances from AE overhead distribution lines: 7.5-ft horizontal sky-to-ground and 15-ft radius for customer facilities; AE survey and written clearance requirements; delineation of clearance envelopes; clearances measured from overhead conductors (not from utility pole centerline)\n    63→- UCM 1.10.5.A — 12-inch minimum clearance from AE underground facilities (cable, conduit, duct structure, pull-boxes) to other utilities at perpendicular crossings; 36-inch from high-pressure gas lines (≥60 psi); joint trench with other utilities requires written AE agreement\n    64→- UCM 1.10.6.1 — 10-foot minimum horizontal clearance from AE poles and overhead lines (other than service drop) for customer service entrance facilities\n    65→- UCM 1.10.6.2 — Service-drop conductor clearances: 12-ft (pedestrian-only), 16-ft (residential driveways and non-truck areas), 18-ft (truck traffic areas)\n    66→- UCM 1.14.3.1 — Transmission line permanent clearance envelope (site-specific, three-dimensional; requires AE engineering determination)\n    67→- UCM 1.14.3.2 — Working clearances from transmission lines during construction (10-ft for 69 kV; 11.33-ft for 138 kV; 15.33-ft for 345 kV)\n    68→- UCM 1.14.4 — Transmission ROW access corridor requirements: 25-ft wide, 16-ft minimum vertical clearance along full ROW length\n    69→- UCM 1.14.9 — Construction requirements near transmission lines: 48-hour pre-construction safety meeting; barricades 10-ft from transmission structures; warning signs; prohibition on materials/spoils in easement; 20-ft crane buffer\n    70→- UCM 1.3.16 — Minimum 16-ft vertical clearance and 12-ft horizontal width for AE truck access routes to AE equipment; 35-ft vertical clearance for equipment set-up areas\n    71→- UCM 2.9.1.A.3 — Prohibition on parallel routing within PUE or utility easement boundaries; only perpendicular or up to 45° from perpendicular crossings permitted\n    72→- UCM 2.9.1.B.2 — Minimum 5-foot horizontal separation between AW infrastructure and all other non-AW infrastructure\n    73→- UCM 2.9.1.B.3 — Vertical separation standards for crossings with AW water/wastewater mains: 12-inch minimum when AW main is above; 18-inch minimum when AW main is below\n    74→- UCM 2.9.1.D — Encasement piping specifications for utilities crossing under roadways and structures\n    75→- UCM 2.9.2.B.17 — Trenchless crossing under water lines: minimum clearance = greater of 2× excavation diameter or 36 inches\n    76→- UCM 2.9.2.B.19 — Bedding and backfill specification (CLSM material) for open-cut crossings under water mains; this is a material specification, not a vertical clearance standard\n    77→- UCM 2.9.4.G.5 — Large-diameter cleanout requirements for wastewater service lines 8–12 inches in diameter; maximum 14-foot depth\n    78→- DCM 1.2.4.E.1.e.ii — 14-ft minimum vertical clearance for SCM maintenance access drives\n    79→- DCM 1.2.4.E.2.e — 14-ft minimum vertical clearance for SCM staging areas\n    80→- LDC Subchapter E, § 2.2.2.B.3 — Overhead utility zone: 10-ft radius from energized conductor, required along Core Transit Corridors; must be delineated in addition to all other sidewalk zones\n    81→- LDC Subchapter E, § 2.2.3.B.3 — Extends Core Transit Corridor overhead utility zone standards to Urban Roadways\n    82→- § 14-11-180(C) — AULCC ROW assignment change approval required when installing a facility within 24 inches of an existing ROW facility (Austin City Code, Title 14)\n    83→\n    84→*External references confirmed via UCM cross-reference (not in Bureau):*\n    85→- OSHA 1910.333 — Electrical safety clearances for unqualified persons near live overhead lines; confirmed via UCM 1.10.2\n    86→- Texas H&S Code § 752.004 — Overhead power line safety clearance requirements; confirmed via UCM 1.10.2\n    87→\n    88→## Documents to Review\n    89→\n    90→- **Utility Plan Sheets** — Overhead utility locations, crossing configurations, easement boundaries, and clearance dimensions from buildings, vaults, FDCs, cleanouts, and storm drain manholes; AE underground conduit routing and joint-trench configurations\n    91→- **Site/Civil Plan Sheets** — Building footprint locations relative to overhead conductor radius zones and delineated overhead utility zones; overhead utility zone graphic shown on plan\n    92→- **Demolition Plan Sheets** — Demolition sequence safety requirements relative to transmission lines; equipment/scaffolding restrictions; warning/caution notes near at-grade and overhead utility appurtenances\n    93→- **Staging and Construction Management Plan** — Staging area and temporary spoils locations relative to utility alignments and transmission easements; vertical clearance dimensions at staging areas and driveway access points; transmission ROW access corridor\n    94→- **General Notes / Construction Specifications** — Field verification requirement for utility separations prior to final acceptance; contractor caution language near at-grade appurtenances\n    95→- **Austin Energy Written Clearance Approval** — Required in project file when vaults are proposed near overhead power lines (UCM 1.10.3)\n    96→- **Austin Energy Survey Documentation** — Required per UCM 1.10.3 when customer facilities are proposed near AE primary voltage facilities; applicant must provide AE with survey showing proximity to AE overhead primary conductors\n    97→- **Texas Gas Services Coordination Documentation** — Required when utilities cross Texas Gas Service lines (2-ft vertical clearance verification; confirm current TGS contact directly)\n    98→\n    99→## Checklist Items\n   100→\n   101→| ID | Deficiency | Code Citation | Applicability |\n   102→|----|------------|---------------|---------------|\n   103→| EL-1.1 | Building or structure located within 10-ft radius of energized overhead conductors, or overhead utility zone not graphically delineated on plans | LDC Subchapter E, § 2.2.2.B.3; LDC Subchapter E, § 2.2.3.B.3 | Only when overhead utilities remain on or adjacent to the site AND the site fronts a Core Transit Corridor or Urban Roadway. CTCs are the 20 enumerated segments (e.g., South Congress Ave north of Stassney, Lamar Blvd from Banyon to Ben White, Guadalupe St, Burnet Rd from 45th to Anderson — full list in definition file). Urban Roadways are roads within the boundary polygon bounded by 183/Hwy 71/Loop 1/Lake Austin/Exposition/35th/RM 2222/Mesa/Spicewood Springs/360/Great Hills Trail/Braker/Burnet, excluding CTCs and Highways. Check: confirm street classification; if applicable, confirm 10-ft radius buffer is graphically shown centered on each energized conductor and no building falls within the zone. UCM 1.10.3 clearances (EL-1.2 and EL-1.3) apply universally regardless of street type. |\n   104→| EL-1.2 | Customer facilities (buildings, parking garages, light poles, signs, billboards, chimneys, antennas, tanks, or other customer installations) not maintaining minimum 7-ft 6-in horizontal sky-to-ground clearance from AE overhead primary, neutral, or secondary conductors | UCM 1.10.3 | Only when overhead electric distribution utilities are present on or adjacent to the site. Check: utility plan for horizontal dimensions from the outside (outermost) conductor to any customer facility edge. Clearance must be from conductor, not pole centerline (see EL-1.5). |\n   105→| EL-1.3 | Customer facilities not maintaining minimum 15-ft radius clearance from AE overhead distribution primary and neutral conductors; or customer facility proposed to be installed under or over AE overhead distribution facilities | UCM 1.10.3 | Only when overhead distribution primary or neutral conductors are present and customer facilities are proposed. Check: utility plan for 15-ft radial clearance from the outside conductor to any customer facility. This threshold applies simultaneously with EL-1.2; both must be satisfied. |\n   106→| EL-1.4 | Vault vertical clearance from top of proposed structure to sag of lowest overhead power line not labeled on plans; or written clearance approval from Austin Energy not obtained or not included in project file | UCM 1.10.3 | Only when a vault is proposed in proximity to overhead power lines. Check: utility plan for labeled vertical clearance dimension from vault top to lowest conductor sag; project file for Austin Energy written clearance approval letter. Violation: plan states \"vault approved by AE Design\" without including the approval letter. |\n   107→| EL-1.5 | Clearance dimensions on plans shown measured from utility pole centerline rather than from the outermost (outside) conductor; clearances appear to comply but are anchored to the pole symbol rather than the conductor position | UCM 1.10.3 | Only when items EL-1.2, EL-1.3, or EL-1.4 apply. Check: verify all clearance dimension lines on the utility plan or clearance exhibit are anchored to the outside conductor. On three-phase or multi-phase configurations, the outside conductor may be several feet from the pole center; pole-anchored dimensions are non-compliant even when stated distances appear adequate. |\n   108→| EL-1.6 | Customer service entrance facilities (service equipment, service conductors, one-point rack, FDC installations, or meter vaults) not maintaining minimum 10-ft horizontal clearance from AE poles and overhead lines other than the service drop; or electrical appurtenances not maintaining 10-ft clearance from live energized overhead lines | UCM 1.10.6.1; UCM 1.10.2 | Only when overhead AE utilities (poles, conductors) are present and customer service entrance facilities, meter vaults, FDCs, or electrical appurtenances are proposed. Check: utility plan for horizontal dimension from service entrance equipment or appurtenances to nearest AE pole or overhead line other than the service drop. |\n   109→| EL-1.7 | Overhead service-drop conductors crossing areas subject to truck traffic (roads, streets, alleys, parking lots, or loading zones with commercial vehicle access) not demonstrating minimum 18-ft vertical clearance from final grade | UCM 1.10.6.2 | Only when overhead service drops cross areas accessible by vehicles. Check: utility plan and site layout for service drop locations and the area type below. Public streets and loading areas always require 18-ft. Residential driveways not subject to truck traffic require 16-ft. Alleys and parking lots require judgment: apply 18-ft unless truck access is affirmatively excluded. Unlabeled or ambiguous areas default to 18-ft. |\n   110→| EL-1.8 | Utilities crossing Texas Gas Service lines not maintaining minimum 2-ft vertical clearance; or coordination with Texas Gas Services not documented in project file | Reviewer Convention | Only when proposed utilities cross Texas Gas Service lines. Check: utility plan for gas crossing locations and project file for TGS coordination documentation confirming 2-ft vertical clearance. Confirm current TGS contact directly; department-provided contact information may be outdated. |\n   111→| EL-1.9 | AE underground facilities (cable, conduit, duct structure, pull-boxes) not maintaining minimum 12-inch clearance — both horizontally and vertically — from other utilities at perpendicular crossings | UCM 1.10.5.A | Only when proposed utilities cross AE underground facilities at perpendicular angles. Check: utility plan and crossing details for labeled clearance dimensions between AE underground conduit or cable and crossing utilities. This rule applies only to perpendicular crossings; parallel runs require a separate written joint-trench agreement (see EL-1.10). |\n   112→| EL-1.10 | Non-AE utilities proposed in joint trench (parallel run) with AE underground conduit or cable without written AE joint-trench agreement referenced on plans or in project file | UCM 1.10.5.A | Only when non-AE utilities are shown running parallel to or in the same trench corridor as AE underground conduit or cable. Check: utility plan for any parallel routing of non-AE utilities along AE underground duct banks or cable runs; confirm project file contains or references a written AE joint-trench agreement. |\n   113→| EL-1.11 | Proposed utility in public ROW shown within 24 inches of an existing ROW facility without AULCC ROW assignment change approval documentation; plans show close-proximity utilities with no notation of AULCC authorization | § 14-11-180(C) | Only when utilities are proposed within public ROW. Check: utility plan for any proposed facility located within 24 inches of an existing ROW facility. The requirement is not a flat prohibition but an approval process: AULCC Completeness Letter (ROW assignment change approval) must be in the project file. Violation pattern: utilities shown at close spacing with no acknowledgment of AULCC review or approval. |\n   114→| EL-1.12 | Utility crossing method (trenchless vs. open-cut) not identified on plans or in crossing details, preventing verification of the applicable separation standard (UCM 2.9.2.B.17 for trenchless — clearance = greater of 2× excavation diameter or 36 in; UCM 2.9.1.B.3 for open-cut — 12-in when AW main above; 18-in when AW main below) | UCM 2.9.2.B.17; UCM 2.9.1.B.3 | Only when utility crossings are proposed over or under water, reclaimed water, or wastewater mains. Check: utility plan and crossing detail sheets for crossing method designation; profile drawings for labeled clearance or separation dimensions. |\n   115→| EL-1.13 | Encasement specifications not provided for utilities crossing under roadways, structures, or other barriers; or bored section design criteria missing from crossing detail sheets | UCM 2.9.1.D | Only when utilities are bored or encased under roads, structures, or barriers. Check: utility plan crossing details and notes for encasement material type, dimensions, and longitudinal extent specifications. |\n   116→| EL-1.14 | Large-diameter cleanouts (serving wastewater service lines 8–12 inches in diameter) not maintaining minimum 5-ft horizontal separation from existing utility poles and guy wires | UCM 2.9.1.B.2 | Only when large-diameter cleanouts are proposed. A large-diameter cleanout is required when the wastewater service line is 8\", 10\", or 12\" in diameter (per UCM 2.9.4.G.5); it is located at the property line or within a 5×5 ft easement. Check: utility and drainage plans for cleanout locations relative to utility poles and guy wires; flag any separation less than 5 feet. |\n   117→| EL-1.15 | Storm drain manholes proposed with less than 5-ft horizontal clearance from Austin Energy overhead electric poles | Reviewer Convention | Only when storm drain manholes are proposed near Austin Energy overhead electric poles. Check: drainage and utility plans for manhole-to-pole horizontal dimensions. |\n   118→| EL-1.16 | Non-electrical utilities (water, wastewater, gas, communications, or other non-AE utilities) routed parallel within electrical or public utility easement boundaries; only perpendicular crossings (or up to 45° from perpendicular) are permitted | UCM 2.9.1.A.3 | Only when utilities are proposed within or adjacent to electrical easements or PUEs. Identify easement boundaries from the plat or easement exhibit; easement labels may read \"electric easement,\" \"AE easement,\" \"transmission easement,\" or \"aerial electric easement.\" Check: utility plan for any non-electrical utility running parallel within the easement rather than crossing perpendicularly. |\n   119→| EL-1.17 | Electric utility poles proposed within water easements or within 10 ft of large electric transmission structures | Requires human verification | Only when utility poles are proposed near water easements or electric transmission structures (69 kV, 138 kV, or 345 kV — identifiable by voltage label, lattice towers or substantially taller structures, long insulators, or labeled transmission easement). ⚠️ The specific prohibition previously attributed to UCM 2.9.1.A.1 cannot be confirmed in current code text — that section governs easement width requirements, not pole placement restrictions. Flag for department review to identify the current applicable provision before issuing a deficiency comment. |\n   120→| EL-1.18 | Demolition plan sequence notes do not address required safety clearances from transmission lines: pre-construction safety meeting ≥48 hours before work, barricades erected 10 ft from all transmission structures (including guys and anchors) within the construction area, warning signs under overhead facilities, and prohibition on materials or spoils storage within the transmission easement | UCM 1.14.9 | Only when demolition activities are proposed near electric transmission lines (69 kV, 138 kV, or 345 kV overhead facilities, or within a labeled transmission easement). Check: demolition plan sequence notes for all four requirements listed in the deficiency. |\n   121→| EL-1.19 | Construction plans do not demonstrate maintenance of a 25-ft-wide access corridor with minimum 16-ft vertical clearance along the full transmission ROW length for the duration of construction | UCM 1.14.4 | Only when construction activities occur adjacent to a transmission ROW or transmission easement. Check: staging and construction plans for a labeled access corridor along the transmission ROW with 25-ft minimum width and 16-ft minimum vertical clearance dimensions. |\n   122→| EL-1.20 | Demolition plan does not include a note requiring contractor to use extreme caution when working near at-grade utility appurtenances | Reviewer Convention | Only when demolition activities are proposed. Check: demolition plan general notes for contractor caution language near at-grade utility appurtenances. |\n   123→| EL-1.21 | Plans do not include a requirement for field verification of all utility separations and clearances during construction and prior to final acceptance | Reviewer Convention | All sites with proposed utilities. Check: general notes or construction specifications on plan set for field verification language. |\n   124→| EL-1.22 | SCM staging area vertical clearance from existing and proposed vegetation and all other objects not demonstrated at minimum 14 ft | DCM 1.2.4.E.2.e | Only when permanent SCM staging areas are required: site contains a COA-maintained or residential development (single-family/duplex) SCM with combined total storage volume >5,000 cu ft OR maximum ponding depth >3 ft. Commercial privately-maintained SCMs are not subject to this requirement unless COA-maintained. Check: staging plan or drainage detail sheets for labeled 14-ft vertical clearance from all objects above the staging area. |\n   125→| EL-1.23 | Maintenance access drive vertical clearance from existing and proposed vegetation and all other objects not demonstrated at minimum 14 ft, including vegetation at maturity | DCM 1.2.4.E.1.e.ii | Only when maintenance access drives are present: site contains a qualifying SCM (all residential development SCMs regardless of maintenance type; all COA-maintained SCMs; commercial SCMs where access from ROW or grade between single-family lots exceeds 20%), above the 5,000 cu ft / 3-ft depth exemption thresholds. Check: plan sheets for labeled 14-ft vertical clearance along maintenance access drive corridors; vegetation canopy heights at maturity must be verified against the 14-ft standard. |\n   126→| EL-1.24 | Work zone or construction access driveway below overhead utilities does not demonstrate required vertical clearance: 18 ft where truck traffic is anticipated; 16 ft minimum for driveways affirmatively restricted to non-truck vehicles | UCM 1.10.6.2; UCM 1.3.16 | Only when work zones or construction access points are adjacent to or below overhead service drops or distribution lines. Check: staging/construction plan for labeled clearance dimensions at driveway access locations below overhead lines; apply 18-ft for any area not affirmatively restricted from truck access. Note: a 15'6\" figure used in prior versions of this item cannot be confirmed in current UCM or AEDC. |\n   127→| EL-1.25 | Staging or temporary spoils areas shown overlapping with proposed utility line alignments or placed within the transmission easement | UCM 1.14.9; Reviewer Convention | Only when staging or temporary spoils areas are proposed. Check: staging/construction plan against utility alignment plan for spatial conflicts; the transmission easement boundary must be kept clear of all construction materials and spoils under any circumstances. |\n   128→| EL-1.26 | Overhead electrical clearance zones not graphically delineated on plans: distribution clearance envelope (7.5-ft horizontal sky-to-ground boundary and 15-ft radial zone from outside conductor) and/or transmission clearance envelope (AE-provided site-specific analysis) not shown on site plan or utility plan sheets | UCM 1.10.3; UCM 1.14.3.1 | Only when overhead electric utilities (distribution or transmission) are present on or adjacent to the site. For distribution: check for graphical representation of both clearance envelopes anchored to the outside conductor, not the pole. For transmission: AE-provided clearance analysis or AE transmission review documentation must be present; transmission clearance zones cannot be independently verified from plan data and require AE engineering. |\n   129→| EL-1.27 | Horizontal dashed height limit lines not shown on plans to delineate AE overhead conductor clearance envelope boundaries where overhead utility hazards are present | UCM 1.10.3 | Only when overhead utilities are present. Check: site plan and utility plan sheets for horizontal height limit line annotations delineating clearance envelope boundaries from overhead conductors. |\n   130→| EL-1.28 | Sloped or stepped dashed height limit lines not shown on plans where overhead utilities are present on sites with non-uniform grade conditions | UCM 1.10.3 | Only when overhead utilities are present on sites with sloped or stepped terrain. Check: grading plan and utility plan sheets for sloped or stepped height limit line annotations that reflect actual grade changes beneath the overhead conductor span. |\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6e121e67-5008-4f73-b228-82e438cca3a3","uuid":"71bcb110-92b8-40af-956a-64c2149e673a","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/wnavey/code/controlroom/conductor/workspace/bureau/jurisdictions/austin/review-guides/el/1.md","content":"# Overhead and Vertical Electrical Clearances\n\n## Description\n\nThis grouping covers minimum clearance requirements between structures, utilities, and energized overhead electrical conductors, as well as minimum separations between utilities at crossings and in parallel routing. It also encompasses vertical clearance requirements for staging areas, maintenance access drives, and work zone operations during construction. Reviewers apply these checks whenever overhead utilities remain on or adjacent to a site, utility crossings are proposed, or construction activities occur near electrical infrastructure.\n\n## Regulatory Overview\n\n**Permanent Clearances from AE Overhead Distribution Lines (UCM 1.10.3)**\n\nCustomer facilities — defined broadly as any customer-side structure, improvement, or installation, including buildings, parking garages, light poles, signs, billboards, chimneys, antennas, tanks, and vaults — must maintain two simultaneous and independent clearances from AE overhead distribution conductors: (1) a **7-foot 6-inch horizontal sky-to-ground clearance** measured from the outermost (outside) conductor, and (2) a **15-foot-radius clearance** from overhead distribution primary and neutral conductors. No customer facility may be installed under or over AE overhead distribution facilities — this prohibition is absolute and separate from the numerical clearance requirements. Where a vault is proposed near overhead power lines, the applicant must provide AE with a survey showing proximity to AE primary voltage facilities, and Austin Energy written clearance approval must be obtained and included in the project file. Both clearance thresholds must be satisfied simultaneously; an installation that clears 7.5 feet horizontally but falls within the 15-foot radial zone still violates UCM 1.10.3.\n\nThe Land Development Code separately requires an **overhead utility zone** — a 10-foot-radius buffer from the energized conductor — to be graphically delineated on site plans when overhead utilities remain on the site. No building or structure may encroach into this zone. This LDC requirement applies **only when the site fronts a Core Transit Corridor or Urban Roadway** (LDC Subchapter E, § 2.2.2.B.3 for CTCs; extended to Urban Roadways by LDC Subchapter E, § 2.2.3.B.3). **Core Transit Corridors** are 20 enumerated street segments: South First St (north of Ben White), E 7th St (west of Pleasant Valley), E/W 5th St (various limits), E/W 6th St (various limits), W 35th/38th St corridor, Airport Blvd (Lamar to I-35), Anderson Ln (Burnet to Mopac), Barton Springs Rd (east of Robert E. Lee), Burnet Rd (45th to Anderson), South Congress Ave (north of Stassney), Guadalupe St, Lamar Blvd (Banyon to Ben White), MLK Jr. Blvd (Pearl to Airport), Riverside Dr (Lamar to E. Ben White/Hwy 71), Cameron Rd (51st to US-290), 51st St (Cameron to Manor), Gaston Place, and Briarcliff Blvd. **Urban Roadways** are roads within a boundary polygon roughly bounded by 183/Hwy 71/Loop 1/Lake Austin/Exposition/35th St/Loop 1/RM 2222/Mesa/Spicewood Springs/360/Great Hills Trail/183/Braker/Burnet, excluding CTCs and Highways. The UCM 1.10.3 clearances (7.5-ft horizontal and 15-ft radius) apply universally to all customer facilities regardless of street type; the LDC 10-ft zone is an additional, street-type-dependent requirement.\n\n**Critical measurement rule:** All clearances must be measured from the **outermost (outside) conductor**, not from the utility pole centerline. On three-phase configurations, the outside conductor may be several feet offset from the pole center. Plans showing clearance dimensions anchored to the pole symbol rather than the conductor are non-compliant even when stated distances appear adequate. This is the single most common measurement error on AE clearance exhibits.\n\n**Service-Drop Clearances and Customer Service Entrance Facilities (UCM 1.10.6.1 and 1.10.6.2)**\n\nService-drop conductors must maintain minimum vertical clearance from final grade based on the area type: **12 ft** over pedestrian-only residential areas; **16 ft** over residential driveways, or parking lots and alleys not subject to truck traffic, and commercial services over pedestrian areas; **18 ft** over roads, streets, alleys, parking lots, and any other area subject to truck traffic. An area is \"subject to truck traffic\" when commercial freight, delivery, refuse trucks, or other heavy commercial vehicles are reasonably expected based on land use. Public streets always require 18 ft. Alleys and parking lots may require either 16-ft or 18-ft depending on use — when ambiguous, apply 18-ft as the conservative default. Customer service entrance facilities (service equipment, service conductors, one-point rack) must also maintain a **10-foot minimum horizontal clearance** from AE poles and overhead lines other than the service drop (UCM 1.10.6.1).\n\n**AE Underground Facility Separations and Utility Easement Routing (UCM 1.10.5.A and UCM 2.9.1.A.3)**\n\nAE underground facilities (cable, conduit, duct structure, pull-boxes) require a **12-inch minimum clearance** — both horizontally and vertically — from other utilities at **perpendicular crossings only**; fuel lines and high-pressure gas (≥60 psi) require 36-inch minimum separation. This rule governs perpendicular crossings only; parallel routing of other utilities alongside AE underground conduit requires a separate written joint-trench agreement from AE. Non-electrical utilities may cross a public utility easement (PUE) or electrical easement only perpendicularly or up to 45° from perpendicular — **parallel routing within easement boundaries is prohibited at all times** (UCM 2.9.1.A.3). Vertical separation standards for crossings with AW water/wastewater mains are: 12-inch minimum when AW main is above; 18-inch minimum when AW main is below (UCM 2.9.1.B.3). For trenchless crossings under water lines, the minimum clearance is the greater of (a) two times the excavation diameter/span or (b) 36 inches (UCM 2.9.2.B.17). UCM 2.9.2.B.19 governs bedding and backfill material (CLSM) for open-cut crossings under water mains — it is a material specification, not an additional vertical clearance standard; the separation standard for open-cut crossings is in UCM 2.9.1.B.3.\n\n**Transmission Line Requirements (UCM 1.14 series)**\n\nTransmission lines are electric facilities at **60 kV or higher** (UCM §1.17.0 Glossary). Austin Energy operates transmission at 69 kV, 138 kV, and 345 kV. Physical indicators: substantially taller poles or lattice towers, long insulators (≥3 ft), conductors spread widely apart, shield wire at top. Transmission clearance envelopes are complex, site-specific, three-dimensional shapes that cannot be reduced to a table; Austin Energy engineering must evaluate specific locations (UCM 1.14.3.1). OSHA working clearances during construction, by voltage: **10 ft for 69 kV; 11.33 ft for 138 kV; 15.33 ft for 345 kV** (UCM 1.14.3.2). Transmission circuits cannot be de-energized for nearby construction, even temporarily. Key construction requirements (UCM 1.14.9): pre-construction safety meeting with AE ≥48 hours before commencement; barricades erected 10 ft from all transmission structures (including guys and anchors) within construction area; warning signs under overhead facilities; no materials storage or spoils within transmission easement; tower cranes and temporary structures positioned so they could not fall within 20 ft of transmission facility. The transmission ROW access corridor must be maintained at 25 ft wide with **16-ft minimum vertical clearance** along the full ROW length (UCM 1.14.4).\n\n**SCM Staging Areas and Maintenance Access Drives (DCM 1.2.4.E)**\n\nPermanent SCM staging areas (required for COA-maintained SCMs and residential single-family/duplex development SCMs) must maintain **14-ft minimum vertical clearance** from existing and proposed vegetation and all other objects (DCM 1.2.4.E.2.e). Maintenance access drives (required for all residential development SCMs, all COA-maintained SCMs, and commercial SCMs where access from ROW or grade exceeds 20%) must maintain the same **14-ft minimum vertical clearance** (DCM 1.2.4.E.1.e.ii). Both requirements include vegetation heights at maturity. Neither requirement applies if combined total SCM storage volume ≤5,000 cu ft AND maximum ponding depth ≤3 ft.\n\n**Key Thresholds:**\n- 10 ft: Overhead utility zone radius from energized conductor (LDC Subchapter E, § 2.2.2.B.3 — CTC/Urban Roadway only)\n- 7.5 ft: Horizontal sky-to-ground clearance from AE overhead conductors for all customer facilities (UCM 1.10.3)\n- 15 ft: Radial clearance from AE overhead distribution primary and neutral conductors (UCM 1.10.3)\n- 10 ft: Minimum horizontal clearance from AE poles for customer service entrance facilities (UCM 1.10.6.1); OSHA working clearance from lines ≤50 kV (UCM 1.10.2)\n- 18 ft: Service-drop clearance over truck traffic areas (UCM 1.10.6.2)\n- 16 ft: Service-drop clearance over residential driveways / non-truck alleys/lots (UCM 1.10.6.2); transmission ROW access corridor vertical clearance (UCM 1.14.4)\n- 12 in: AE underground facility separation from other utilities at perpendicular crossings (UCM 1.10.5.A)\n- 18 in / 12 in: Water/wastewater main crossing separation — AW main above / AW main below (UCM 2.9.1.B.3)\n- 36 in (or 2× excavation diameter): Trenchless crossing clearance from water line (UCM 2.9.2.B.17)\n- 14 ft: SCM staging area and maintenance access drive vertical clearance (DCM 1.2.4.E.2.e; DCM 1.2.4.E.1.e.ii)\n- 24 in: ROW facility proximity requiring AULCC ROW assignment change approval (§ 14-11-180(C))\n\n**Exceptions:**\n- SCM staging area and maintenance access drive clearance requirements exempt when combined pond volume ≤5,000 cu ft AND ponding depth ≤3 ft\n- AW may approve reduced crossing vertical separations under constrained conditions (6-in vs. 12-in; 12-in vs. 18-in)\n- LDC overhead utility zone (10-ft radius) applies only on Core Transit Corridors and Urban Roadways; UCM 1.10.3 permanent clearances apply universally regardless of street type\n\n**Common Violation Patterns:**\n- Clearances dimensioned from utility pole centerline rather than from the outermost conductor\n- Plan states \"vault approved by AE Design\" without including the approval letter in the project file\n- Proposed utility within 24 inches of existing ROW facility with no AULCC approval or deviation documentation\n- Non-electrical utilities running parallel within an electrical or PUE easement boundary\n- Maintenance access drive shown with tree canopy overhead where canopy at maturity would reduce clearance below 14 ft, or no clearance labeled\n- AE underground conduit in joint trench with other utilities without written AE agreement\n\n## Code References\n\n- UCM 1.10.2 — Working clearances from energized overhead electric utility lines; incorporates OSHA 1910.333 (10-ft from lines ≤50 kV for unqualified persons) and Texas H&S Code § 752.004 requirements by reference\n- UCM 1.10.3 — Permanent clearances from AE overhead distribution lines: 7.5-ft horizontal sky-to-ground and 15-ft radius for customer facilities; AE survey and written clearance requirements; delineation of clearance envelopes; clearances measured from overhead conductors (not from utility pole centerline)\n- UCM 1.10.5.A — 12-inch minimum clearance from AE underground facilities (cable, conduit, duct structure, pull-boxes) to other utilities at perpendicular crossings; 36-inch from high-pressure gas lines (≥60 psi); joint trench with other utilities requires written AE agreement\n- UCM 1.10.6.1 — 10-foot minimum horizontal clearance from AE poles and overhead lines (other than service drop) for customer service entrance facilities\n- UCM 1.10.6.2 — Service-drop conductor clearances: 12-ft (pedestrian-only), 16-ft (residential driveways and non-truck areas), 18-ft (truck traffic areas)\n- UCM 1.14.3.1 — Transmission line permanent clearance envelope (site-specific, three-dimensional; requires AE engineering determination)\n- UCM 1.14.3.2 — Working clearances from transmission lines during construction (10-ft for 69 kV; 11.33-ft for 138 kV; 15.33-ft for 345 kV)\n- UCM 1.14.4 — Transmission ROW access corridor requirements: 25-ft wide, 16-ft minimum vertical clearance along full ROW length\n- UCM 1.14.9 — Construction requirements near transmission lines: 48-hour pre-construction safety meeting; barricades 10-ft from transmission structures; warning signs; prohibition on materials/spoils in easement; 20-ft crane buffer\n- UCM 1.3.16 — Minimum 16-ft vertical clearance and 12-ft horizontal width for AE truck access routes to AE equipment; 35-ft vertical clearance for equipment set-up areas\n- UCM 2.9.1.A.3 — Prohibition on parallel routing within PUE or utility easement boundaries; only perpendicular or up to 45° from perpendicular crossings permitted\n- UCM 2.9.1.B.2 — Minimum 5-foot horizontal separation between AW infrastructure and all other non-AW infrastructure\n- UCM 2.9.1.B.3 — Vertical separation standards for crossings with AW water/wastewater mains: 12-inch minimum when AW main is above; 18-inch minimum when AW main is below\n- UCM 2.9.1.D — Encasement piping specifications for utilities crossing under roadways and structures\n- UCM 2.9.2.B.17 — Trenchless crossing under water lines: minimum clearance = greater of 2× excavation diameter or 36 inches\n- UCM 2.9.2.B.19 — Bedding and backfill specification (CLSM material) for open-cut crossings under water mains; this is a material specification, not a vertical clearance standard\n- UCM 2.9.4.G.5 — Large-diameter cleanout requirements for wastewater service lines 8–12 inches in diameter; maximum 14-foot depth\n- DCM 1.2.4.E.1.e.ii — 14-ft minimum vertical clearance for SCM maintenance access drives\n- DCM 1.2.4.E.2.e — 14-ft minimum vertical clearance for SCM staging areas\n- LDC Subchapter E, § 2.2.2.B.3 — Overhead utility zone: 10-ft radius from energized conductor, required along Core Transit Corridors; must be delineated in addition to all other sidewalk zones\n- LDC Subchapter E, § 2.2.3.B.3 — Extends Core Transit Corridor overhead utility zone standards to Urban Roadways\n- § 14-11-180(C) — AULCC ROW assignment change approval required when installing a facility within 24 inches of an existing ROW facility (Austin City Code, Title 14)\n\n*External references confirmed via UCM cross-reference (not in Bureau):*\n- OSHA 1910.333 — Electrical safety clearances for unqualified persons near live overhead lines; confirmed via UCM 1.10.2\n- Texas H&S Code § 752.004 — Overhead power line safety clearance requirements; confirmed via UCM 1.10.2\n\n## Documents to Review\n\n- **Utility Plan Sheets** — Overhead utility locations, crossing configurations, easement boundaries, and clearance dimensions from buildings, vaults, FDCs, cleanouts, and storm drain manholes; AE underground conduit routing and joint-trench configurations\n- **Site/Civil Plan Sheets** — Building footprint locations relative to overhead conductor radius zones and delineated overhead utility zones; overhead utility zone graphic shown on plan\n- **Demolition Plan Sheets** — Demolition sequence safety requirements relative to transmission lines; equipment/scaffolding restrictions; warning/caution notes near at-grade and overhead utility appurtenances\n- **Staging and Construction Management Plan** — Staging area and temporary spoils locations relative to utility alignments and transmission easements; vertical clearance dimensions at staging areas and driveway access points; transmission ROW access corridor\n- **General Notes / Construction Specifications** — Field verification requirement for utility separations prior to final acceptance; contractor caution language near at-grade appurtenances\n- **Austin Energy Written Clearance Approval** — Required in project file when vaults are proposed near overhead power lines (UCM 1.10.3)\n- **Austin Energy Survey Documentation** — Required per UCM 1.10.3 when customer facilities are proposed near AE primary voltage facilities; applicant must provide AE with survey showing proximity to AE overhead primary conductors\n- **Texas Gas Services Coordination Documentation** — Required when utilities cross Texas Gas Service lines (2-ft vertical clearance verification; confirm current TGS contact directly)\n\n## Checklist Items\n\n| ID | Deficiency | Code Citation | Applicability |\n|----|------------|---------------|---------------|\n| EL-1.1 | Building or structure located within 10-ft radius of energized overhead conductors, or overhead utility zone not graphically delineated on plans | LDC Subchapter E, § 2.2.2.B.3; LDC Subchapter E, § 2.2.3.B.3 | Only when overhead utilities remain on or adjacent to the site AND the site fronts a Core Transit Corridor or Urban Roadway. CTCs are the 20 enumerated segments (e.g., South Congress Ave north of Stassney, Lamar Blvd from Banyon to Ben White, Guadalupe St, Burnet Rd from 45th to Anderson — full list in definition file). Urban Roadways are roads within the boundary polygon bounded by 183/Hwy 71/Loop 1/Lake Austin/Exposition/35th/RM 2222/Mesa/Spicewood Springs/360/Great Hills Trail/Braker/Burnet, excluding CTCs and Highways. Check: confirm street classification; if applicable, confirm 10-ft radius buffer is graphically shown centered on each energized conductor and no building falls within the zone. UCM 1.10.3 clearances (EL-1.2 and EL-1.3) apply universally regardless of street type. |\n| EL-1.2 | Customer facilities (buildings, parking garages, light poles, signs, billboards, chimneys, antennas, tanks, or other customer installations) not maintaining minimum 7-ft 6-in horizontal sky-to-ground clearance from AE overhead primary, neutral, or secondary conductors | UCM 1.10.3 | Only when overhead electric distribution utilities are present on or adjacent to the site. Check: utility plan for horizontal dimensions from the outside (outermost) conductor to any customer facility edge. Clearance must be from conductor, not pole centerline (see EL-1.5). |\n| EL-1.3 | Customer facilities not maintaining minimum 15-ft radius clearance from AE overhead distribution primary and neutral conductors; or customer facility proposed to be installed under or over AE overhead distribution facilities | UCM 1.10.3 | Only when overhead distribution primary or neutral conductors are present and customer facilities are proposed. Check: utility plan for 15-ft radial clearance from the outside conductor to any customer facility. This threshold applies simultaneously with EL-1.2; both must be satisfied. |\n| EL-1.4 | Vault vertical clearance from top of proposed structure to sag of lowest overhead power line not labeled on plans; or written clearance approval from Austin Energy not obtained or not included in project file | UCM 1.10.3 | Only when a vault is proposed in proximity to overhead power lines. Check: utility plan for labeled vertical clearance dimension from vault top to lowest conductor sag; project file for Austin Energy written clearance approval letter. Violation: plan states \"vault approved by AE Design\" without including the approval letter. |\n| EL-1.5 | Clearance dimensions on plans shown measured from utility pole centerline rather than from the outermost (outside) conductor; clearances appear to comply but are anchored to the pole symbol rather than the conductor position | UCM 1.10.3 | Only when items EL-1.2, EL-1.3, or EL-1.4 apply. Check: verify all clearance dimension lines on the utility plan or clearance exhibit are anchored to the outside conductor. On three-phase or multi-phase configurations, the outside conductor may be several feet from the pole center; pole-anchored dimensions are non-compliant even when stated distances appear adequate. |\n| EL-1.6 | Customer service entrance facilities (service equipment, service conductors, one-point rack, FDC installations, or meter vaults) not maintaining minimum 10-ft horizontal clearance from AE poles and overhead lines other than the service drop; or electrical appurtenances not maintaining 10-ft clearance from live energized overhead lines | UCM 1.10.6.1; UCM 1.10.2 | Only when overhead AE utilities (poles, conductors) are present and customer service entrance facilities, meter vaults, FDCs, or electrical appurtenances are proposed. Check: utility plan for horizontal dimension from service entrance equipment or appurtenances to nearest AE pole or overhead line other than the service drop. |\n| EL-1.7 | Overhead service-drop conductors crossing areas subject to truck traffic (roads, streets, alleys, parking lots, or loading zones with commercial vehicle access) not demonstrating minimum 18-ft vertical clearance from final grade | UCM 1.10.6.2 | Only when overhead service drops cross areas accessible by vehicles. Check: utility plan and site layout for service drop locations and the area type below. Public streets and loading areas always require 18-ft. Residential driveways not subject to truck traffic require 16-ft. Alleys and parking lots require judgment: apply 18-ft unless truck access is affirmatively excluded. Unlabeled or ambiguous areas default to 18-ft. |\n| EL-1.8 | Utilities crossing Texas Gas Service lines not maintaining minimum 2-ft vertical clearance; or coordination with Texas Gas Services not documented in project file | Reviewer Convention | Only when proposed utilities cross Texas Gas Service lines. Check: utility plan for gas crossing locations and project file for TGS coordination documentation confirming 2-ft vertical clearance. Confirm current TGS contact directly; department-provided contact information may be outdated. |\n| EL-1.9 | AE underground facilities (cable, conduit, duct structure, pull-boxes) not maintaining minimum 12-inch clearance — both horizontally and vertically — from other utilities at perpendicular crossings | UCM 1.10.5.A | Only when proposed utilities cross AE underground facilities at perpendicular angles. Check: utility plan and crossing details for labeled clearance dimensions between AE underground conduit or cable and crossing utilities. This rule applies only to perpendicular crossings; parallel runs require a separate written joint-trench agreement (see EL-1.10). |\n| EL-1.10 | Non-AE utilities proposed in joint trench (parallel run) with AE underground conduit or cable without written AE joint-trench agreement referenced on plans or in project file | UCM 1.10.5.A | Only when non-AE utilities are shown running parallel to or in the same trench corridor as AE underground conduit or cable. Check: utility plan for any parallel routing of non-AE utilities along AE underground duct banks or cable runs; confirm project file contains or references a written AE joint-trench agreement. |\n| EL-1.11 | Proposed utility in public ROW shown within 24 inches of an existing ROW facility without AULCC ROW assignment change approval documentation; plans show close-proximity utilities with no notation of AULCC authorization | § 14-11-180(C) | Only when utilities are proposed within public ROW. Check: utility plan for any proposed facility located within 24 inches of an existing ROW facility. The requirement is not a flat prohibition but an approval process: AULCC Completeness Letter (ROW assignment change approval) must be in the project file. Violation pattern: utilities shown at close spacing with no acknowledgment of AULCC review or approval. |\n| EL-1.12 | Utility crossing method (trenchless vs. open-cut) not identified on plans or in crossing details, preventing verification of the applicable separation standard (UCM 2.9.2.B.17 for trenchless — clearance = greater of 2× excavation diameter or 36 in; UCM 2.9.1.B.3 for open-cut — 12-in when AW main above; 18-in when AW main below) | UCM 2.9.2.B.17; UCM 2.9.1.B.3 | Only when utility crossings are proposed over or under water, reclaimed water, or wastewater mains. Check: utility plan and crossing detail sheets for crossing method designation; profile drawings for labeled clearance or separation dimensions. |\n| EL-1.13 | Encasement specifications not provided for utilities crossing under roadways, structures, or other barriers; or bored section design criteria missing from crossing detail sheets | UCM 2.9.1.D | Only when utilities are bored or encased under roads, structures, or barriers. Check: utility plan crossing details and notes for encasement material type, dimensions, and longitudinal extent specifications. |\n| EL-1.14 | Large-diameter cleanouts (serving wastewater service lines 8–12 inches in diameter) not maintaining minimum 5-ft horizontal separation from existing utility poles and guy wires | UCM 2.9.1.B.2 | Only when large-diameter cleanouts are proposed. A large-diameter cleanout is required when the wastewater service line is 8\", 10\", or 12\" in diameter (per UCM 2.9.4.G.5); it is located at the property line or within a 5×5 ft easement. Check: utility and drainage plans for cleanout locations relative to utility poles and guy wires; flag any separation less than 5 feet. |\n| EL-1.15 | Storm drain manholes proposed with less than 5-ft horizontal clearance from Austin Energy overhead electric poles | Reviewer Convention | Only when storm drain manholes are proposed near Austin Energy overhead electric poles. Check: drainage and utility plans for manhole-to-pole horizontal dimensions. |\n| EL-1.16 | Non-electrical utilities (water, wastewater, gas, communications, or other non-AE utilities) routed parallel within electrical or public utility easement boundaries; only perpendicular crossings (or up to 45° from perpendicular) are permitted | UCM 2.9.1.A.3 | Only when utilities are proposed within or adjacent to electrical easements or PUEs. Identify easement boundaries from the plat or easement exhibit; easement labels may read \"electric easement,\" \"AE easement,\" \"transmission easement,\" or \"aerial electric easement.\" Check: utility plan for any non-electrical utility running parallel within the easement rather than crossing perpendicularly. |\n| EL-1.17 | Electric utility poles proposed within water easements or within 10 ft of large electric transmission structures | Requires human verification | Only when utility poles are proposed near water easements or electric transmission structures (69 kV, 138 kV, or 345 kV — identifiable by voltage label, lattice towers or substantially taller structures, long insulators, or labeled transmission easement). ⚠️ The specific prohibition previously attributed to UCM 2.9.1.A.1 cannot be confirmed in current code text — that section governs easement width requirements, not pole placement restrictions. Flag for department review to identify the current applicable provision before issuing a deficiency comment. |\n| EL-1.18 | Demolition plan sequence notes do not address required safety clearances from transmission lines: pre-construction safety meeting ≥48 hours before work, barricades erected 10 ft from all transmission structures (including guys and anchors) within the construction area, warning signs under overhead facilities, and prohibition on materials or spoils storage within the transmission easement | UCM 1.14.9 | Only when demolition activities are proposed near electric transmission lines (69 kV, 138 kV, or 345 kV overhead facilities, or within a labeled transmission easement). Check: demolition plan sequence notes for all four requirements listed in the deficiency. |\n| EL-1.19 | Construction plans do not demonstrate maintenance of a 25-ft-wide access corridor with minimum 16-ft vertical clearance along the full transmission ROW length for the duration of construction | UCM 1.14.4 | Only when construction activities occur adjacent to a transmission ROW or transmission easement. Check: staging and construction plans for a labeled access corridor along the transmission ROW with 25-ft minimum width and 16-ft minimum vertical clearance dimensions. |\n| EL-1.20 | Demolition plan does not include a note requiring contractor to use extreme caution when working near at-grade utility appurtenances | Reviewer Convention | Only when demolition activities are proposed. Check: demolition plan general notes for contractor caution language near at-grade utility appurtenances. |\n| EL-1.21 | Plans do not include a requirement for field verification of all utility separations and clearances during construction and prior to final acceptance | Reviewer Convention | All sites with proposed utilities. Check: general notes or construction specifications on plan set for field verification language. |\n| EL-1.22 | SCM staging area vertical clearance from existing and proposed vegetation and all other objects not demonstrated at minimum 14 ft | DCM 1.2.4.E.2.e | Only when permanent SCM staging areas are required: site contains a COA-maintained or residential development (single-family/duplex) SCM with combined total storage volume >5,000 cu ft OR maximum ponding depth >3 ft. Commercial privately-maintained SCMs are not subject to this requirement unless COA-maintained. Check: staging plan or drainage detail sheets for labeled 14-ft vertical clearance from all objects above the staging area. |\n| EL-1.23 | Maintenance access drive vertical clearance from existing and proposed vegetation and all other objects not demonstrated at minimum 14 ft, including vegetation at maturity | DCM 1.2.4.E.1.e.ii | Only when maintenance access drives are present: site contains a qualifying SCM (all residential development SCMs regardless of maintenance type; all COA-maintained SCMs; commercial SCMs where access from ROW or grade between single-family lots exceeds 20%), above the 5,000 cu ft / 3-ft depth exemption thresholds. Check: plan sheets for labeled 14-ft vertical clearance along maintenance access drive corridors; vegetation canopy heights at maturity must be verified against the 14-ft standard. |\n| EL-1.24 | Work zone or construction access driveway below overhead utilities does not demonstrate required vertical clearance: 18 ft where truck traffic is anticipated; 16 ft minimum for driveways affirmatively restricted to non-truck vehicles | UCM 1.10.6.2; UCM 1.3.16 | Only when work zones or construction access points are adjacent to or below overhead service drops or distribution lines. Check: staging/construction plan for labeled clearance dimensions at driveway access locations below overhead lines; apply 18-ft for any area not affirmatively restricted from truck access. Note: a 15'6\" figure used in prior versions of this item cannot be confirmed in current UCM or AEDC. |\n| EL-1.25 | Staging or temporary spoils areas shown overlapping with proposed utility line alignments or placed within the transmission easement | UCM 1.14.9; Reviewer Convention | Only when staging or temporary spoils areas are proposed. Check: staging/construction plan against utility alignment plan for spatial conflicts; the transmission easement boundary must be kept clear of all construction materials and spoils under any circumstances. |\n| EL-1.26 | Overhead electrical clearance zones not graphically delineated on plans: distribution clearance envelope (7.5-ft horizontal sky-to-ground boundary and 15-ft radial zone from outside conductor) and/or transmission clearance envelope (AE-provided site-specific analysis) not shown on site plan or utility plan sheets | UCM 1.10.3; UCM 1.14.3.1 | Only when overhead electric utilities (distribution or transmission) are present on or adjacent to the site. For distribution: check for graphical representation of both clearance envelopes anchored to the outside conductor, not the pole. For transmission: AE-provided clearance analysis or AE transmission review documentation must be present; transmission clearance zones cannot be independently verified from plan data and require AE engineering. |\n| EL-1.27 | Horizontal dashed height limit lines not shown on plans to delineate AE overhead conductor clearance envelope boundaries where overhead utility hazards are present | UCM 1.10.3 | Only when overhead utilities are present. Check: site plan and utility plan sheets for horizontal height limit line annotations delineating clearance envelope boundaries from overhead conductors. |\n| EL-1.28 | Sloped or stepped dashed height limit lines not shown on plans where overhead utilities are present on sites with non-uniform grade conditions | UCM 1.10.3 | Only when overhead utilities are present on sites with sloped or stepped terrain. Check: grading plan and utility plan sheets for sloped or stepped height limit line annotations that reflect actual grade changes beneath the overhead conductor span. |","numLines":130,"startLine":1,"totalLines":130}}}
