{"level":30,"time":1776954336062,"pid":39427,"hostname":"Williams-MacBook-Pro.local","step":"review-runs","item":"1.md","index":3,"runIndex":"run-1","type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_01DMzCxKLRTTRGbFj8MZosUx","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_013XXhjYmAZSbmYd7Bje1b6V","name":"mcp__conductor_tools__vision","input":{"documentId":"1144b126-1216-4dee-a89c-178950fa7777","sheetNum":3,"prompt":"This is the General Notes sheet for a townhomes development. Please examine and describe: 1) What are the key electric transmission notes and safety requirements shown? 2) Are there any specific dimensions or clearance measurements referenced for work near transmission lines? 3) What construction sequence or safety protocol is described? 4) Are there any specific notes about transmission easement access or width requirements? 5) Are there demolition or construction sequence notes that address transmission line safety? 6) What are the specific requirements for staging areas, aerial equipment, or work near overhead utilities? Please read and transcribe any key sections related to electrical transmission or clearance requirements."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":7,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3625,"cache_read_input_tokens":60628,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3625},"output_tokens":6,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"51587e83-ba72-4647-83cb-6d408affd332","uuid":"b5a43abb-85c6-416e-8c7b-3cb5e2312a13"}
{"level":30,"time":1776954336063,"pid":39427,"hostname":"Williams-MacBook-Pro.local","step":"review-runs","item":"1.md","index":3,"runIndex":"run-1","documentId":"1144b126-1216-4dee-a89c-178950fa7777","sheetNum":3,"prompt":"This is the General Notes sheet for a townhomes development. Please examine and describe: 1) What are the key electric transmission notes and safety requirements shown? 2) Are there any specific dimensions or clearance measurements referenced for work near transmission lines? 3) What construction sequence or safety protocol is described? 4) Are there any specific notes about transmission easement access or width requirements? 5) Are there demolition or construction sequence notes that address transmission line safety? 6) What are the specific requirements for staging areas, aerial equipment, or work near overhead utilities? Please read and transcribe any key sections related to electrical transmission or clearance requirements.","msg":"Calling vision tool: 1144b126-1216-4dee-a89c-178950fa7777 (3)"}
{"level":30,"time":1776954337227,"pid":39427,"hostname":"Williams-MacBook-Pro.local","step":"review-runs","item":"3.md","index":7,"runIndex":"run-3","documentId":"1144b126-1216-4dee-a89c-178950fa7777","sheetNum":3,"prompt":"Examine this General Notes sheet for Austin Energy (AE) plan notes. Specifically, look for:\n\n1. Are the four Standard Austin Energy Notes present? These should include:\n   - Note about AE's right to prune/remove trees to keep easements clear and compliance with LDC Chapter 25-8, Subchapter B\n   - Note about owner/developer obligation to provide easements/access for overhead and underground AE facilities, and facility compliance with LDC Chapter 25-8\n   - Note about owner responsibility for erosion control, revegetation, tree protection, and tree pruning/removal within 10 feet of proposed overhead electrical facility centerlines\n   - Note about owner responsibility for maintaining clearances required by NESC, OSHA, City rules, and Texas state laws near overhead power lines\n\n2. Is there a note stating the site lies within the Austin Energy service area?\n\n3. Is there a note stating the developer is responsible for designing and constructing conduit and related infrastructure to current Austin Energy Electric Service Standards?\n\n4. Are there any notes about transmission easements? Look for references to clearances, access, easement requirements, or road load capacity requirements.\n\n5. Are there any preconstruction meeting requirements mentioned?\n\nList all electric-related notes verbatim as they appear on the sheet, organized by category.","res":{"steps":[{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Based on a detailed visual analysis of the provided Site Plan Release Notes sheet, here are the answers to your questions regarding Austin Energy (AE) plan notes:\n\n**1. Are the four Standard Austin Energy Notes present?**\nYes. The four standard Austin Energy notes are present exactly as requested. They are listed under the heading **\"Electric Notes\"** in the middle column of the sheet (numbered 1 through 4). *Note: The exact same text is also repeated as an unnumbered paragraph block further down the page, between the \"Fire Department\" and \"Sequence of Construction\" sections.*\n\n**2. Is there a note stating the site lies within the Austin Energy service area?**\nNo. There is no note present on this sheet stating that the site lies within the Austin Energy service area.\n\n**3. Is there a note stating the developer is responsible for designing and constructing conduit and related infrastructure to current Austin Energy Electric Service Standards?**\nNo. This specific requirement regarding the design and construction of conduit/infrastructure is not present on this sheet.\n\n**4. Are there any notes about transmission easements?**\nYes. There is a large, dedicated section titled **\"Electric Transmission Notes\"** containing 16 numbered items. This section covers clearances (e.g., prohibitions on planting, digging, grading, or placing structures within 25 feet), access requirements, and road load capacity. Specifically, Note 12 addresses load capacity, requiring that roads/driveways/paving within the easement be built to support a 46,000 lb tandem axle. \n\n**5. Are there any preconstruction meeting requirements mentioned?**\nYes. A pre-construction safety meeting is explicitly required in **\"Electric Transmission Notes\" (Note 3)**, which requires contacting Andrew Perez 48 hours before commencement to schedule a tailgate safety meeting.\n\n---\n\n### **Verbatim Transcription of Electric-Related Notes**\n\n**Electric Notes**\n1. Austin Energy has the right to prune and/or remove trees, shrubbery and other obstructions to the extent necessary to keep the easements clear. Austin Energy will perform all tree work in compliance with Chapter 25-8, Subchapter B of the City of Austin Land Development Code.\n2. The owner/developer of this subdivision/lot shall provide Austin Energy with any easement and/or access required, in addition to those indicated, for the installation and ongoing maintenance of overhead and underground electric facilities. These easements and/or access are required to provide electric service to the building and will not be located so as to cause the site to be out of compliance with Chapter 25-8 of the City of Austin Land Development Code.\n3. The owner shall be responsible for installation of temporary erosion control, revegetation and tree protection. In addition, the owner shall be responsible for any initial tree pruning and tree removal that is within ten feet of the center line of the proposed overhead electric facilities designed to provide electric service to this project. The owner shall include Austin Energy's work within the limits of construction for this project.\n4. The owner of the property is responsible for maintaining clearances required by the National Electric Safety Code, Occupational Safety and Health Administration (OSHA) regulations, City of Austin rules and regulations and Texas state laws pertaining to clearances when working in close proximity to overhead power lines and equipment. Austin Energy will not render electric service unless required clearances are maintained. All costs incurred because of failure to comply with the required clearances will be charged to the owner.\n5. Any relocation of electric facilities shall be at the landowner's/developer's expense.\n\n**Electric Transmission Notes**\n1. No trees shall be planted within the transmission easement. No trees shall be planted within 25 feet of the base of the transmission structure. Vehicular access for Austin Energy trucks and equipment is always to be maintained within the easement. Austin Energy will not be responsible for damage and/or removal of vegetation within the easement.\n2. Do not dig or grade within 25 feet of the transmission structures, including down guy anchors. Grading near electric transmission facilities must be coordinated with Austin Energy prior to commencement of grading. Call Andrew Perez at 512.505.7133 to schedule a meeting.\n3. A pre-construction safety meeting is required with Austin Energy 48 hours before commencement of construction. Call Andrew Perez at 512.505.7133 to schedule a tailgate safety meeting. Include Chuck Hendry (at 505-7151) in the meeting, if cranes are being used during construction. OSHA requires a 20' clearance from energized transmission lines during construction.\n4. Barricades must be erected 10 feet from electric transmission structures during construction.\n5. Any relocation of electric transmission facilities or outages caused by this project will be charged to the property owner and contractor.\n6. Warning signs must be placed under the overhead electric transmission facilities as notification of the electrical hazard.\n7. For safety reasons, aerial equipment, dumpsters, staging or spoils areas are not permitted within 20 feet of the transmission wire and/or structures and must be located outside the easement.\n8. 24 hour access to electric facilities shall be maintained.\n9. Any temporary or permanent fence preventing access to the transmission facilities and/or easement shall be coordinated with Austin Energy staff. AE staff will install a lock on the gate for access.\n10. Property owner and contractor are responsible for dust controls to minimize contamination of wire and insulators caused by dust from this project. Any subsequent cleaning or electrical outages caused by dust from this project will be charged to the property owner and contractor.\n11. Property owner is responsible for any damages to curbing, landscaping, walls, paving placed around the electric transmission structures/powerlines caused by Austin Energy during maintenance and repairs. All curbing within the electric transmission easement must be roll/river curbing.\n12. Roads/driveways/pavement/parking within the easement should be built to support a 46,000 lb tandem axle (5 foot spacing) to ensure safety.\n13. Fire hydrants must be located out of the transmission easement and a minimum of 20 feet from any transmission structure.\n14. Sprinklers are prohibited within 25 feet of transmission poles and structures.\n15. Do not dig or grade within 25 feet of the transmission structures, including down guy anchors)\n16. Owner may not place, erect, construct or maintain the following within electric transmission easement:\n    - any permanent structures, including, but not limited to habitable structures such as homes, mobile homes, garages, or offices.\n    - any structure of any kind in such proximity to the electric transmission or distribution lines, poles, structures, towers, or appurtenant facilities as would constitute a violation of the National Electric Safety Code in effect at the time the structure is erected, nor\n    - any structures, including but not limited to, fences, storage sheds, drainage, filtration or detention ponds which would impair Austin Energy's access to the transmission easements or its lines, poles, structures, towers or appurtenant facilities in the easements.","providerMetadata":{"google":{"thoughtSignature":""}}}],"finishReason":"stop","usage":{"inputTokens":1581,"outputTokens":1499,"totalTokens":7414,"reasoningTokens":4334},"warnings":[],"request":{"body":{"prompt":[{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"file","mediaType":"image/jpeg","data":"[BINARY_DATA_REMOVED - length: 3237656]"},{"type":"text","text":"# Your task\n\nYou are analyzing a subset of a real estate development site plan submission document.\n\nVisually analyze the binary file provided with extreme accuracy. Pay close attention to tables, reproducing them EXACTLY as they appear, character-by-character.\n\nCRITICAL: Do not paraphrase, interpret, or \"fill in\" text. If you cannot read something clearly, state \"text unclear\" rather than guessing. Transcribe field labels and values exactly as written.\n\nIf the requirements being validated require certain content to be present, look closely for it and provide all relevant information that pertains to the broader request. Remember that you are only looking at a single supplementary document, or a single page from the primary site plan. Just because you do not find evidence for a requirement on this page does not mean it will not be present on another page.\n\nReport back precisely on what you see, using exact quotes for any text you transcribe from forms, tables, or labels.\n\nThe final review agent will not be able to look at this document directly, so you must be their eyes. Ensure you describe everything relevant in full detail with perfect accuracy.\n"},{"type":"text","text":"Examine this General Notes sheet for Austin Energy (AE) plan notes. Specifically, look for:\n\n1. Are the four Standard Austin Energy Notes present? These should include:\n   - Note about AE's right to prune/remove trees to keep easements clear and compliance with LDC Chapter 25-8, Subchapter B\n   - Note about owner/developer obligation to provide easements/access for overhead and underground AE facilities, and facility compliance with LDC Chapter 25-8\n   - Note about owner responsibility for erosion control, revegetation, tree protection, and tree pruning/removal within 10 feet of proposed overhead electrical facility centerlines\n   - Note about owner responsibility for maintaining clearances required by NESC, OSHA, City rules, and Texas state laws near overhead power lines\n\n2. Is there a note stating the site lies within the Austin Energy service area?\n\n3. Is there a note stating the developer is responsible for designing and constructing conduit and related infrastructure to current Austin Energy Electric Service Standards?\n\n4. Are there any notes about transmission easements? Look for references to clearances, access, easement requirements, or road load capacity requirements.\n\n5. Are there any preconstruction meeting requirements mentioned?\n\nList all electric-related notes verbatim as t