{"level":30,"time":1776954449523,"pid":39427,"hostname":"Williams-MacBook-Pro.local","step":"review-runs","item":"1.md","index":6,"runIndex":"run-2","type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_013JurzuEmm51DshXGENPtKi","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Good! So from the plat, the easements shown are:\n1. 5' P.U.E. (Public Utility Easement) - northern boundary of Lot 1 and Lot 2\n2. 5' Elec. Ease. (Electric Easement) - southern boundary of Lot 1\n3. 5' P.U.E. - southern boundary of Lot 2 (eastern portion)\n4. 26' Drainage Easement (Existing) - northern section of Lot 2\n5. 10' Wastewater Ease. - running north-south through middle of Lot 2\n\nBUT - there is NO transmission easement explicitly labeled on the Mecey Subdivision plat (Sheet 2).\n\nHowever, Sheet 3 Block 20 clearly references transmission facilities and transmission easements, with 16 detailed notes about working near them.\n\nLet me now think about what's happening:\n1. Sheet 3 refers to \"Electric Transmission Notes\" and \"transmission easement\" and \"transmission structures\"\n2. Facts.md mentions various easements but doesn't explicitly mention transmission\n3. The plat shows only utility easements, drainage, wastewater, electric, and PUE - not transmission\n\nLooking back at the note references in facts.md, I see:\n- Easement 3 — 10' Wastewater Easement (Travis County Doc 73/76, Case C8S-75-160) \n- Easement 4 — 25' Drainage Easement (from Cortez Heights plat, Travis County Doc 64/13, Case C8-71-079)\n- Easement 5 — 7.5' Public Utility Easement (from Cortez Heights plat)\n- Easement 6 — 10' Drainage Easement (from Trico Subd plat)\n\n\n- Easement 7 & 8 — 5' Electric Easements (×2) from Mecey plat\n- Easement 9 — 7.5' Public Utility Easement from Mecey plat\n\nThe transmission facilities mentioned in Sheet 3 could originate from the adjacent plats (Cortez Heights or Trico) or potentially run along Valley View Road itself in the public right-of-way. Since Valley View Road is designated as a Local Street under ASMP Level 1, transmission lines might be accommodated there rather than crossing through the private property.\n\nHowever, Sheet 3 Block 20 explicitly references a transmission easement affecting the site with restrictions on building placement. This suggests there is indeed an easement on the property itself, separate from any lines in the public right-of-way. Now I need to work through the checklist items to determine which standards actually apply.\n\nFor EL-1.1 regarding the 10-foot overhead utility zone delineation: since Valley View Road qualifies as a Local Street rather than a CTC or Urban Roadway, the LDC overhead utility zone requirement doesn't apply here. For EL-1.2 and EL-1.3 regarding customer service lines, I need to evaluate those next. The transformers and meters serving customer facilities need to maintain proper clearances from AE poles and overhead lines—checking if they're positioned within the required 10-ft buffer. Service drops appear to be underground based on the electrical plan, so overhead service-drop requirements don't apply. Gas utility crossings need verification to ensure they're not present on the site. For underground electrical infrastructure, I need to confirm clearances at utility crossings and determine whether joint-trench agreements are necessary with AE for any shared conduit runs.\n\nThe electrical plan indicates water, wastewater, and storm drain lines crossing or running parallel to electrical lines, so those interactions require verification. Public ROW spacing along Valley View Road must meet the 24-inch clearance standard shown on the utility tap plan. Drainage, water, and wastewater crossings need review, along with staging area and construction access clearances from transmission facilities. The overhead clearance envelope lacks graphical delineation and height limit lines on the current plans.\n\nI need to systematically verify these items by examining the utility tap sheets for underground crossings and ROW utilities, checking the drainage sheets for SCM staging clearances, reviewing the demolition plan for transmission facility clearances, and confirming the staging and construction plan details.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":7,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1456,"cache_read_input_tokens":56279,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1456},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"401e6f81-2db8-4fbd-be98-34356fe0baff","uuid":"fedcb2ef-d1cc-42f7-b376-67d0c6852978"}
