{"level":30,"time":1776954471363,"pid":39427,"hostname":"Williams-MacBook-Pro.local","step":"review-runs","item":"1.md","index":6,"runIndex":"run-2","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01SayDzy3AuKnPUu5ReGMP5p","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# Sheet 5 — Content Blocks\n     2→\n     3→## Block 1: drawing — This is a detailed site plan drawing showing the layout of multiple buildings, parking areas, utility lines, and erosion control measures. Key features include building footprints with square footage and finished floor elevations (FFE), contour lines, trees, and specific environmental protection measures like silt fences, mulch socks, and tree protection areas. It also shows adjacent properties, roads, and easements.\n     4→\n     5→The image displays a comprehensive civil engineering site plan. \n     6→\n     7→Key elements include:\n     8→- Multiple building footprints (e.g., Bldg. 1, 3,290 sf, FFE = 687.50; Bldg. 2, 4,830 sf, FFE = 685.50).\n     9→- Topographic contour lines with elevations (e.g., 680, 681, 682).\n    10→- Existing and proposed utility lines, including wastewater and water lines.\n    11→- Trees marked with circles, some designated for removal and others for protection.\n    12→- Erosion control measures indicated by specific line types and symbols, such as:\n    13→  - Silt fences (SF)\n    14→  - Mulch socks (MS)\n    15→  - Tree protection (Typ.)\n    16→  - Inlet protection (Typ.)\n    17→- Designated areas for 'Stabilized Construction Entrance', 'Concrete Truck Cleanout', and 'Staging and Storage Area'.\n    18→- Adjacent properties with zoning and use information (e.g., 'Lot A Cortex Heights 9.81 Acres Zoning: MF 3-CO Use: Multi-family').\n    19→- Easements, such as '15' Drainage Easement' and '5' Elec. & Tele. Easement'.\n    20→- Roadways, including 'Valley View Road 60' R.O.W.'\n    21→- A retaining wall and concrete buttresses are also shown.\n    22→\n    23→## Block 2: notes — Provides the legal description of the property as recorded in the Travis County Plat Records.\n    24→\n    25→**Legal Description**\n    26→Lot 1, Mecey Subdivision Volume 73, Page 76, Plat Records of Travis County, Texas.\n    27→\n    28→## Block 3: notes — Detailed instructions regarding soil decompaction and topsoil requirements for areas to be revegetated or landscaped, referencing specific local development codes and environmental criteria manuals.\n    29→\n    30→1. Per LDC 25-8-323(C), for areas on the site that are to remain pervious after development, any soils that are compacted during site grading and construction operations must be decompacted in compliance with the ECM and in compliance with SSM 661S.\n    31→2. All disturbed areas to be revegetated are required to have a minimum of six (6) inches of topsoil. Topsoil shall meet the definition in Standard Specification 601S.3. In areas where no topsoil exists, or where topsoil is needed for vegetative establishment, the subgrade shall be loosened by discing or scarifying to a depth of at least two (2) inches prior to placement of six (6) inches of topsoil. [ECM 1.4.7, Appendix P-1]\n    32→3. Permanent vegetative stabilization of areas disturbed by construction shall be per ECM Appendix P-1, unless otherwise indicated on the plan.\n    33→4. For parking-lot islands, medians, peninsulas, and similar landscape areas that are to receive parking-lot stormwater runoff, finished elevation must be at least six (6) inches below the finished curb elevation to allow for placement of six (6) inches of topsoil [ECM 1.4.7].\n    34→\n    35→## Block 4: notes — This block provides a legend for the symbols and line types used in the site plan drawing, specifically for erosion control measures and construction limits.\n    36→\n    37→**Legend**\n    38→\n    39→| Symbol | Description |\n    40→| :--- | :--- |\n    41→| [Solid thick black line] | Proposed Limits of Construction |\n    42→| [Line with 'SF'] | Proposed Silt Fence |\n    43→| [Line with triangles] | Proposed Triangular Sediment Filter Dike |\n    44→| [Line with 'MS'] | Proposed Mulch Sock |\n    45→| [Line with small rectangles] | Proposed Tree Protection Fence |\n    46→| [Filled square] | Proposed Inlet Protection |\n    47→| [Circle with 'X'] | Tree to be Removed |\n    48→\n    49→## Block 5: notes — A brief note directing the reader to another sheet for the comprehensive tree list.\n    50→\n    51→**Tree List**\n    52→Refer to landscape plan sheet 30 for the tree list.\n    53→\n    54→## Block 7: notes — This section details the location and elevation of two benchmarks used for the project, both of which are Mag Nails set in asphalt.\n    55→\n    56→**Benchmarks**\n    57→\n    58→B.M. #1 - Mag Nail Set in Asphalt\n    59→Elevation = 678.52' NAVD 88 (GEOID 18)\n    60→\n    61→B.M. #2 - Mag Nail Set in Asphalt\n    62→Elevation = 681.08' NAVD 88 (GEOID 18)\n    63→\n    64→## Block 11: text_block — This block contains the title of the plan, the project name, and the project address. It identifies the document as an 'Erosion and Sedimentation Control Plan' for the 'Valley View Townhomes' project located in Austin, Texas.\n    65→\n    66→Erosion and Sedimentation\n    67→Control Plan\n    68→\n    69→Valley View Townhomes\n    70→3759 Valley View Road\n    71→Austin, Texas 78704\n    72→Travis County\n    73→\n    74→## Block 12: text_block — This section contains the logo, name, address, contact information, and registration number for Bleyl Engineering, the firm responsible for the planning, design, and management of the project. It also lists the cities where they have offices.\n    75→\n    76→Bleyl Engineering\n    77→Planning • Design • Management\n    78→7701 San Felipe Blvd., Suite 200, Austin TX 78729\n    79→Texas Firm Registration No. F-678\n    80→Tel. 512-454-2400\n    81→www.bleylengineering.com\n    82→\n    83→Austin | Bryan | Conroe | Houston\n    84→\n    85→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"401e6f81-2db8-4fbd-be98-34356fe0baff","uuid":"70a349db-e729-4e61-8157-54a65e3656a0","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/wnavey/code/controlroom/conductor/workspace/projects/63cead15-41f8-418c-b0ef-bd5c2b44719a/primary-site-plan/sheet-05/blocks.md","content":"# Sheet 5 — Content Blocks\n\n## Block 1: drawing — This is a detailed site plan drawing showing the layout of multiple buildings, parking areas, utility lines, and erosion control measures. Key features include building footprints with square footage and finished floor elevations (FFE), contour lines, trees, and specific environmental protection measures like silt fences, mulch socks, and tree protection areas. It also shows adjacent properties, roads, and easements.\n\nThe image displays a comprehensive civil engineering site plan. \n\nKey elements include:\n- Multiple building footprints (e.g., Bldg. 1, 3,290 sf, FFE = 687.50; Bldg. 2, 4,830 sf, FFE = 685.50).\n- Topographic contour lines with elevations (e.g., 680, 681, 682).\n- Existing and proposed utility lines, including wastewater and water lines.\n- Trees marked with circles, some designated for removal and others for protection.\n- Erosion control measures indicated by specific line types and symbols, such as:\n  - Silt fences (SF)\n  - Mulch socks (MS)\n  - Tree protection (Typ.)\n  - Inlet protection (Typ.)\n- Designated areas for 'Stabilized Construction Entrance', 'Concrete Truck Cleanout', and 'Staging and Storage Area'.\n- Adjacent properties with zoning and use information (e.g., 'Lot A Cortex Heights 9.81 Acres Zoning: MF 3-CO Use: Multi-family').\n- Easements, such as '15' Drainage Easement' and '5' Elec. & Tele. Easement'.\n- Roadways, including 'Valley View Road 60' R.O.W.'\n- A retaining wall and concrete buttresses are also shown.\n\n## Block 2: notes — Provides the legal description of the property as recorded in the Travis County Plat Records.\n\n**Legal Description**\nLot 1, Mecey Subdivision Volume 73, Page 76, Plat Records of Travis County, Texas.\n\n## Block 3: notes — Detailed instructions regarding soil decompaction and topsoil requirements for areas to be revegetated or landscaped, referencing specific local development codes and environmental criteria manuals.\n\n1. Per LDC 25-8-323(C), for areas on the site that are to remain pervious after development, any soils that are compacted during site grading and construction operations must be decompacted in compliance with the ECM and in compliance with SSM 661S.\n2. All disturbed areas to be revegetated are required to have a minimum of six (6) inches of topsoil. Topsoil shall meet the definition in Standard Specification 601S.3. In areas where no topsoil exists, or where topsoil is needed for vegetative establishment, the subgrade shall be loosened by discing or scarifying to a depth of at least two (2) inches prior to placement of six (6) inches of topsoil. [ECM 1.4.7, Appendix P-1]\n3. Permanent vegetative stabilization of areas disturbed by construction shall be per ECM Appendix P-1, unless otherwise indicated on the plan.\n4. For parking-lot islands, medians, peninsulas, and similar landscape areas that are to receive parking-lot stormwater runoff, finished elevation must be at least six (6) inches below the finished curb elevation to allow for placement of six (6) inches of topsoil [ECM 1.4.7].\n\n## Block 4: notes — This block provides a legend for the symbols and line types used in the site plan drawing, specifically for erosion control measures and construction limits.\n\n**Legend**\n\n| Symbol | Description |\n| :--- | :--- |\n| [Solid thick black line] | Proposed Limits of Construction |\n| [Line with 'SF'] | Proposed Silt Fence |\n| [Line with triangles] | Proposed Triangular Sediment Filter Dike |\n| [Line with 'MS'] | Proposed Mulch Sock |\n| [Line with small rectangles] | Proposed Tree Protection Fence |\n| [Filled square] | Proposed Inlet Protection |\n| [Circle with 'X'] | Tree to be Removed |\n\n## Block 5: notes — A brief note directing the reader to another sheet for the comprehensive tree list.\n\n**Tree List**\nRefer to landscape plan sheet 30 for the tree list.\n\n## Block 7: notes — This section details the location and elevation of two benchmarks used for the project, both of which are Mag Nails set in asphalt.\n\n**Benchmarks**\n\nB.M. #1 - Mag Nail Set in Asphalt\nElevation = 678.52' NAVD 88 (GEOID 18)\n\nB.M. #2 - Mag Nail Set in Asphalt\nElevation = 681.08' NAVD 88 (GEOID 18)\n\n## Block 11: text_block — This block contains the title of the plan, the project name, and the project address. It identifies the document as an 'Erosion and Sedimentation Control Plan' for the 'Valley View Townhomes' project located in Austin, Texas.\n\nErosion and Sedimentation\nControl Plan\n\nValley View Townhomes\n3759 Valley View Road\nAustin, Texas 78704\nTravis County\n\n## Block 12: text_block — This section contains the logo, name, address, contact information, and registration number for Bleyl Engineering, the firm responsible for the planning, design, and management of the project. It also lists the cities where they have offices.\n\nBleyl Engineering\nPlanning • Design • Management\n7701 San Felipe Blvd., Suite 200, Austin TX 78729\nTexas Firm Registration No. F-678\nTel. 512-454-2400\nwww.bleylengineering.com\n\nAustin | Bryan | Conroe | Houston\n\n","numLines":85,"startLine":1,"totalLines":85}}}
