{"level":30,"time":1776954493619,"pid":39427,"hostname":"Williams-MacBook-Pro.local","step":"review-runs","item":"2.md","index":5,"runIndex":"run-2","type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_01ECJ7CYRRxv5sTRYa3dC9Rq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Wait, there's a discrepancy in the vision output. Sheet 31 should be the landscape plan but the vision tool returned \"Sheet 28 of 35\" in the result. Let me check - the README showed 39 sheets total in the site plan, but the output says 35. This might be a different document or older version. The key information I got is still valid for the landscape plan content.\n\nKey findings from Sheet 31 (Landscape Plan):\n1. **Street trees in ROW:** Yes, there are trees shown in the street yard (H-2 area) along Valley View Road with \"SY/M\" labels\n2. **Utility zones/root barriers:** NO explicit markings of utility exclusion zones, root barriers, or planting zone boundaries\n3. **Notes on planting zones/utility gaps:** NONE found\n4. **Utilities relative to landscaping:** \n   - Overhead Electric (OHE) line runs along south boundary and passes through 7+ tree canopies!\n   - Storm Drain (SD) runs through street yard area near landscaping trees\n   - Water (W) and Electric (E) lines run through central portions\n\nThis is significant because:\n- EL-2.11 requires: \"Austin Water mains, services, or appurtenances are proposed within the designated ROW planting zone\"\n- But the vision analysis shows NO explicitly designated planting zones (root barriers with 7-ft setback, utility gaps with 8-ft width) are marked on the landscape plan\n- However, there ARE street trees shown in the ROW\n- The lack of explicit utility zone designations means we can't determine if AW utilities comply with UCM § 2.9.1.C\n\nAlso, the OHE (overhead electric) line running through tree canopies is concerning - but EL-2.9 deals with building setbacks from overhead utilities, not tree interactions.\n\nLet me now check the plat (Sheet 2) to see what easements are shown, and verify the current easement status. \n\nI need to cross-reference facts.md to identify which easements are missing from the site plan documentation. According to the discrepancies section, two critical easements aren't depicted: the Greenbelt Access & Use Easement (Instrument 2010070731) covering 0.31 acres in the NE interior as a perpetual no-build zone, and the City of Austin Drainage Easement (Instrument 2011021137) at 0.130 acres in the NE corner. Both are absent from the site plan and COA GIS records. There are additional GIS-confirmed easements that still need verification against the submitted site plan. - Wastewater Easement Doc# (text cut off) - Sidewalk Easement Doc# (appears twice) - 5' P.U.E. Vol. 69, Pg. 14 Vol. 73, Pg. 76 - 25' Drainage Easement Vol. 73, Pg. 76 - 5' Electric Easement Vol. 69, Pg 14 Vol. 73, Pg. 76 - Existing Access & P.U. Easement Doc. 2010070237 - 5' Elec. Easement Vol. 69, Pg 14, Vol. 73, Pg. 76 - 5' Elec. & Tele. Easement Vol. 5468, Pg. 883\n\nSeveral key easements from the facts are still unaccounted for on the site plan, including the Greenbelt Access & Use Easement (Instrument 2\n\n010070731) and City of Austin Drainage Easement (Instrument 2011021137). These two missing easements cover approximately 0.44 acres in the northeast portion of the site—roughly 19% of the total area—which represents a significant deficiency under EL-2.15 and EL-2.16 standards. Now I need to determine which EL-2.x compliance items actually apply to this site, starting with EL-2.1 regarding underground utilities, which doesn't apply since the property isn't on a CTC or Urban Roadway and falls outside the Subchapter E boundary.\n\nFor EL-2.2, I should verify whether the site is within 1000 feet of Hill Country Roadways, as this would trigger the underground utility requirement. Items EL-2.3 through EL-2.7 don't apply—the site isn't on a CTC, Urban Roadway, or within the Plaza Saltillo TOD area, so those specific corridor and transit-oriented development standards are not relevant.\n\nEL-2.9 becomes a concern if overhead utilities remain on the property, requiring the building to maintain adequate clearance. The detention pond routing appears compliant since utilities bypass rather than run beneath it. Street trees are present but without formally designated planting zones, I can't verify EL-2.11 compliance. Exclusion zones aren't marked on the plan, making EL-2.12 inapplicable. For transformers, the site lacks parkland—only deficient area designation—so EL-2.13 likely doesn't apply.\n\nLight rail proximity isn't a factor here. The critical issue emerges with utility easements: their boundaries aren't clearly positioned relative to the proposed right-of-way, and two essential easements are missing from the documentation entirely, creating potential failures for both EL-2.15 and EL-2.16. Without these easements depicted, I cannot confirm whether items within them are properly licensed. Private utilities in public right-of-way cannot be verified with current detail levels. Demolished building utilities don't appear to be blocking clearances. Public utilities in the right-of-way lack sufficient evidence to determine termination status.\n\nNo underground storage tanks exist on the site. Power pole relocation lacks coordination documentation. Transformer pad dimensions aren't provided, and only one pad shows an easement notation—this creates verification gaps. Tree wells aren't specifically identified on the plans. Since the site falls outside Subchapter E requirements, is under three acres, and uses garage parking rather than surface parking, utility routing through parking doesn't apply.\n\nThe electrical plan needs clarification on whether utilities run underground or overhead—the vision report references an \"Underground Electric Line\" but also shows an \"Overhead Electric (OHE)\" line along the south boundary on Sheet 31, which could trigger EL-2.9 requirements if buildings are positioned nearby. Additionally, Valley View Road doesn't match any Hill Country Roadways listed in the standards (Loop 360, RM 620, RM 2222, RM 2244, Southwest Parkway), so I need to verify the site maintains the required 1000-foot distance from all applicable roads. The most significant concerns appear to be the two missing easements—Greenbelt and City of Austin Drainage—which together cover approximately 19% of the site and aren't shown on the site plan. Looking at the site plan, the detention pond occupies the NE corner—I need to verify whether it's positioned within the easement boundaries or if any structures encroach into those restricted areas. The facts show two distinct easements: the Greenbelt Access & Use Easement (0.31 acres in the NE interior) and the City Drainage Easement (0.13 acres at the NE corner), neither of which appear on the submitted plans. This absence is critical—if the pond or any improvements overlap these undepicted easement zones without proper authorization, the design fails compliance.\n\nFor EL-2.16, the violation only applies when non-utility items sit within utility easements. Since the easement boundaries aren't shown on the site plan, I cannot definitively determine whether buildings, paving, or landscaping encroach into these areas. This creates a verification gap: the applicant hasn't demonstrated that proposed improvements avoid or are properly licensed within these recorded easements.\n\nEL-2.15 addresses a different scenario—easements encroaching into proposed public right-of-way—but the standards also require checking whether easement exhibits document proper licensing per the municipal code. The common violation pattern involves site plans showing structures or improvements within easement boundaries without corresponding License Agreements from the utility or property owner.\n\nHere, the two recorded easements aren't depicted on the site plan at all, making it impossible to verify whether any proposed work falls within them or has proper authorization. This creates a compliance gap under EL-2.16 since the deficiency is not verifiable. Regarding the transformer pads, only one near Building 8 carries a vault easement label, while the other three lack specific easement documentation, which may require further review against the utility standards. dedicated AE easements. Since the site plan doesn't show pad dimensions, clearance measurements, or easement boundaries for three of the four transformer locations, I can't verify compliance with AE Design specifications or the required setbacks outlined in UCM § 1.10.4. The applicability criteria require confirmation of pad sizing, adequate clearances (10 ft from buildings on hot-stick side, 3 ft on non-operator sides, 5 ft on operator/control sides), service access, and proper easement encumbrance—none of which are documented on the drawings provided. property within the site boundaries shown on Sheet 21. However, I can't confirm that AE easements encumber all the pads—only one transformer pad displays a \"Vault Easement\" label, while the other three lack any visible easement designation on the electrical plan. This makes it impossible to verify compliance with the requirement that dedicated AE easements must encumber each pad. I should check Sheet 2 (the plat) to determine if easement information appears there instead.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":7,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3133,"cache_read_input_tokens":65627,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3133},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b6f63193-ad83-4c2c-8030-773d2a7b74d6","uuid":"54eacd0f-070e-4a43-87e3-e8286fd15b70"}
