ransmission clearance envelope (site-specific analysis required from AE or AE-provided transmission review documentation). Overhead Electric (OHE) lines are shown along the site perimeters but clearance envelopes are not delineated."},{"deficiencyId":"EL-1.27","status":"fail","codeCitations":["UCM 1.10.3"],"applicableAreas":["site plan and utility plan sheets where overhead utilities are present"],"sheetReferences":[{"documentId":"1144b126-1216-4dee-a89c-178950fa7777","sheetNumber":9},{"documentId":"1144b126-1216-4dee-a89c-178950fa7777","sheetNumber":21}],"documentReferences":[],"comment":"Horizontal dashed height limit lines are not shown on the site plan or utility plan sheets to delineate the clearance envelope boundaries from overhead conductors. Height limit line annotations showing the 7.5-ft horizontal and 15-ft radial clearance limits from the outside conductor must be added to visually represent the overhead utility hazard zone boundaries."},{"deficiencyId":"EL-1.28","status":"fail","codeCitations":["UCM 1.10.3"],"applicableAreas":["grading plan and utility plan sheets where overhead utilities exist on sites with sloped terrain"],"sheetReferences":[{"documentId":"1144b126-1216-4dee-a89c-178950fa7777","sheetNumber":11}],"documentReferences":[],"comment":"Site has non-uniform grade conditions (topography varies from approximately 678 ft to 687 ft NAVD 88 across the site) with overhead utilities present on western and southern perimeters. No sloped or stepped dashed height limit lines are shown on the grading or utility plan sheets to reflect actual grade changes beneath the overhead conductor span. Sloped or stepped height limit line annotations must be added to account for grade variations."}]},"fast_mode_state":"off","uuid":"ca83fd08-fb48-4514-834c-c884e3acef51"}
{"level":30,"time":1776954724188,"pid":39427,"hostname":"Williams-MacBook-Pro.local","step":"review-runs","item":"2.md","index":5,"runIndex":"run-2","type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_01NUgRuvawcsP4WTkZHcfsgq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Critical finding! The historical plat (Sheet 2, recorded 1975) shows easements on Lot 2, but the current project (2025) is developing Lot 1 (facts.md confirms: \"Legal Description: Lot 1, Mecey Subdivision, Volume 73, Page 76\").\n\nThe vision result shows that on the historical plat:\n- Lot 1 has: 5' P.U.E. on north boundary, 5' Elec. Ease on south boundary\n- Lot 2 has: 26' Drainage Easement, 10' Wastewater Line Easement, other electric easements\n\nBut facts.md indicates there are ADDITIONAL easements affecting Lot 1 that are NOT on the Mecey Subdivision plat:\n\nFrom facts.md:\n**Greenbelt Access & Use Easement (Instrument 2010070731)** - filed 2010-05-18\n- Not shown on Mecey Subdivision plat (which is from 1975)\n- Covers NE interior of Lot 1\n- Not depicted on current site plan\n\n**City of Austin Drainage Easement (Instrument 2011021137)** - filed 2011-02-11\n- Not shown on Mecey Subdivision plat (1975)\n- Covers NE corner of Lot 1\n- Not depicted on current site plan\n\nSo these are LATER easements (2010-2011) that are not on the 1975 plat, and they're also not depicted on the current 2025 site plan.\n\nAlso from facts.md:\n**Easement 3 — 10' Wastewater Easement**\n- Travis County Doc 73/76, Case C8S-75-160 (Mecey Subdivision plat)\n- In COA GIS\n\n\n- Not confirmed depicted on site plan\n\nThere are also two easements from the Cortez Heights plat (Travis County Doc 64/13, Case C8-71-079) that cross the NE boundary of Lot 1: a 25' Drainage Easement and a 7.5' Public Utility Easement, neither of which have been verified on the current site plan.\n\nLooking at Sheet 9 of the current site plan, I can identify several easements that are actually recorded: a 25' Drainage Easement (Vol. 73, Pg. 76), a 5' PUE (Vol. 69, Pg. 14 and Vol. 73, Pg. 76), multiple 5' Electric Easements, and an Existing Access & PU Easement (Doc. 2010070237). However, there's a significant gap in what's being documented versus what should be there.\n\nTwo major easements are completely absent from the site plan: the Greenbelt Access & Use Easement (Instrument 2010070731) and the City of Austin Drainage Easement (Instrument 2011021137). These two instruments alone restrict approximately 19% of the northeastern portion of the site as public easements with strict no-build limitations. This omission creates direct violations of EL-2.15 and EL-2.16—the former addresses utility easement conflicts with public right-of-way, while the latter covers items improperly placed within easement areas without proper licensing. The failure to show these recorded easements on the site plan prevents proper evaluation of what can actually be built on the property. The core issue is that the site plan doesn't depict two recorded easements, making it impossible to determine whether proposed structures, paving, or landscaping fall within those boundaries and have proper licensing. Without the easement boundaries shown, I can't verify compliance with the licensing requirements under UCM § 1.10.10.3 or § 14-11-41. The two undepicted easements—the Greenbelt Access & Use Easement (Instrument 2010070731) and City of Austin Drainage Easement (Instrument 2011021137)—affect the NE portion of the site, particularly the Unit 501 area and detention pond. This makes EL-2.16 not-verifiable as submitted.\n\nFor EL-2.22, the transformer pad situation presents a similar verification problem. While one pad near Building 8 shows an easement label, the other three pads lack dedicated AE easement boundaries on the plan. Additionally, the electrical plan doesn't dimension the required clearances—the 10-foot hot-stick side and 3-foot non-operator sides specified in the code. Without Austin Energy coordination documentation and these dimensional details, I cannot confirm compliance with UCM § 1.10.4 and § 1.5.2.6 for the pads near Buildings 2 and 4.\n\nEL-2.11 raises another verification gap regarding AW utilities in ROW planting zones. The landscape plan shows street trees along Valley View Road in the ROW street yard areas, but the plans lack explicit root barrier boundaries, planting zone designations, and Utility Gap widths. I need these marked designations to verify the 7-foot minimum setback from tree trunks, 2-foot clearance from AW utilities, and 8-foot minimum Utility Gap width required by UCM § 2.9.1.C, particularly along the Valley View Road frontage. The applicability hinges on whether designated planting zones with root barrier boundaries actually exist on the landscape plan. Since none are explicitly marked, the planting zones aren't formally designated per code requirements—which itself constitutes a deficiency. Looking at the utilities routed near the street yard trees, I can't definitively confirm whether Austin Water lines specifically fall within what would be a designated zone, so verification is incomplete.\n\nThe storm drain line runs through the street yard area adjacent to the landscaping trees before reaching Valley View Road, but without marked planting zone boundaries, I cannot determine if this violates the requirement or if Austin Water utilities are even proposed in that location.\n\nChecking Sheet 31 more carefully, the Water and Electric lines appear routed through central portions under the building and paved areas rather than the street yard perimeter. This suggests EL-2.11 may not apply since Austin Water utilities don't seem to be in the ROW street yard zone where the trees are located. I need to identify what actually constitutes a deficiency by examining the violation patterns more closely.\n\nThe grouping notes that planting zone violations occur when AW mains or services route through root barrier lines within planting zones instead of utility gaps. However, the landscape plan shows street trees in the ROW but without formally designated planting zones marked by root barriers. Additionally, if AW utilities aren't even routed through this area, the condition triggering the violation may not exist. The grouping also clarifies that if no ROW street trees exist or no planting zone is formally designated, the item doesn't apply. Since explicit planting zone designations with marked root barriers aren't shown on the plans, I can't verify whether this is actually a deficiency.\n\nFor EL-2.16, the issue centers on whether non-utility items are placed within recorded utility easements without proper licensing. The two critical easements—Greenbelt and City of Austin Drainage—aren't depicted on the site plan, making it impossible to determine if any structures, paving, landscaping, or retaining walls fall within them.\n\nHowever, the facts indicate the City of Austin Drainage easement occupies the NE corner where the detention pond is proposed. Without the easement boundaries explicitly shown, I can't definitively confirm overlap, though the spatial proximity suggests potential conflict. This makes it technically NOT-VERIFIABLE, though there's a strong indication of non-compliance that warrants clarification.\n\nFor EL-2.16, the appropriate status is NOT-VERIFIABLE with a note that the recorded easements need to be depicted on the site plan to allow proper verification. Moving to EL-2.22, the deficiency centers on transformer pad sizing, clearances, and easement requirements. The site plan shows pad locations but lacks dimensioning for the pads themselves, clearances are not specified, and only one of four pads has an easement label. The AE Design specifications control pad dimensions based on customer requirements, so without those specifications provided or referenced, I cannot assess whether the pads meet the required standards.\n\nThe key verification points are whether the pads sit on customer property and whether AE easements properly encumber them. One pad clearly shows an easement marking, but the other three don't, which raises questions about whether they're covered by formal easements or fall within a general electric service easement. The applicability note requires confirmation that an AE easement encumbers the pad, so I need to determine if all four pads have adequate easement coverage to satisfy this requirement.\n\nLooking at the relevant code sections, pads within private AE easements need a License Agreement before installation. The access and clearance standards in the UCM address placement and truck access requirements, but don't necessarily mandate visible easement markings. Since the facts don't reference any AE coordination letters or pad sizing confirmations, and only one of four pads shows a marked easement, I can't definitively verify this item. The missing easement documentation for three pads and lack of AE coordination evidence makes this **NOT-VERIFIABLE**.\n\nNow checking which standards actually apply: Valley View Road is a local street outside the Subchapter E boundary, so EL-2.1 doesn't apply. EL-2.2 requires checking if the site falls within 1000 feet of Hill Country Roadways—Valley View Road itself isn't one, but I need to verify proximity to others. EL-2.3 and EL-2.4 don't apply since the site isn't on CTC or Urban Roadway corridors. Plaza Saltillo TOD requirements also don't apply to this location.\n\nFor the remaining standards: EL-2.8 doesn't apply outside the NBG district. EL-2.9 requires checking if overhead utilities exist and whether buildings sit within 10 feet of them. EL-2.10 appears satisfied since utilities route around the pond rather than underneath. EL-2.11 can't be verified without marked planting zones on the site plan. EL-2.12 doesn't apply without labeled exclusion zones. EL-2.13 doesn't apply since there's no dedicated parkland on the property.\n\nMoving forward, EL-2.14 is not applicable given the absence of light rail infrastructure nearby. EL-2.15 needs verification of whether recorded easements would conflict with the proposed right-of-way. EL-2.16 cannot be verified because critical easements aren't shown on the plans. EL-2.17 requires checking if private utilities are being routed through public right-of-way. EL-2.18 likely doesn't apply as no abandoned utilities have been identified. EL-2.19 needs clarification on whether existing utilities require removal. EL-2.20 is not applicable without underground storage tanks on site. EL-2.21 regarding power pole relocation cannot be verified without coordination letters. EL-2.22 on transformer pad details is not verifiable due to incomplete documentation of clearances and easements. EL-2.23 doesn't apply as tree wells aren't specifically called out. EL-2.24 is not applicable since the site falls outside Subchapter E and is under 3 acres.\n\nFor the Hill Country Roadway criterion, the site at 3759 Valley View Road in West Bouldin Creek is located south of downtown Austin, with Loop 360 to the north and RM 620, RM 2222, and RM 2244 all positioned to the north and northwest.\n\nRegarding EL-2.9 and overhead utilities, Sheet 31 shows an overhead electric line running along the south property boundary. However, the buildings occupy the north and central portions of the site, so I need to determine whether any structures fall within the required 10-foot setback from this line. The criterion only applies to CTC, Urban Roadway, Plaza Saltillo TOD, or NBG frontages, and Valley View Road functions as a local street rather than meeting these classifications.\n\nFor EL-2.16, two recorded easements—the Greenbelt Access & Use Easement (Instrument 2010070731) and the City of Austin Drainage Easement (Instrument 2011021137)—are absent from the site plan. Without their depiction, I cannot confirm whether proposed structures, paving, or landscaping comply with easement restrictions or licensing requirements under UCM § 1.10.10.3 or § 14-11-41. The affected areas include the northeast portion of the site near Unit 501 and the detention pond.\n\nFor EL-2.22, the electrical plan shows four transformer pads, but only one near Building 8 displays an AE/Vault Easement label. The remaining three lack dedicated easement boundaries required by code. Additionally, the plan omits required clearance dimensions (10-ft hot-stick side, 3-ft/5-ft non-operator sides per UCM § 1.10.4) and truck access specifications (12-ft width, 16-ft clearance, 20×\n\n35 ft setup area). Pad sizing per AE Design specifications isn't documented, affecting the transformer locations near Buildings 2, 4/5, 6/7, and 8 shown on Sheet 21.\n\nFor EL-2.11, street trees appear in the ROW street yard area along Valley View Road on the landscape plan, but designated planting zones with marked root barriers per UCM § 2.9.1.C aren't explicitly shown. I cannot confirm the 7-ft minimum setback from tree trunk or 2-ft clearance from AW utilities.\n\nThe core issue is whether Austin Water mains, services, or appurtenances are actually proposed within the designated ROW planting zone. The landscape plan shows the trees, but without marked planting zone boundaries and root barriers, I can't verify if AW utilities fall within that zone or meet the 8-ft minimum Utility Gap width requirement. The applicability hinges on all three elements being present—trees in ROW, designated planting zones with barriers, and AW utilities within those zones—and the documentation doesn't clearly establish this relationship. So the core issue is that while street trees are present in the ROW, there's no way to determine if Austin Water utilities fall within those tree zones since the planting zone boundaries aren't defined. The deficiency specifically concerns AW utilities positioned in designated planting areas, and without marked root barriers or zone delineations, I can't assess whether this condition actually applies. Additionally, if AW utilities do exist near the trees, UCM § 2.9.1.C mandates root barriers be placed—but their absence from the plan makes compliance verification impossible. The most defensible conclusion is that EL-2.11 remains NOT-VERIFIABLE because the landscape plan lacks the necessary zone designations and root barrier markings needed to evaluate whether AW infrastructure conflicts with street tree protection requirements.\n\nWith that settled, my three NOT-VERIFIABLE determinations appear justified: EL-2.16 regarding unlicensed easement items, EL-2.22 for transformer pad clearances, and EL-2.11 for AW utilities in ROW planting zones. Everything else should fall into either N/A or PASS categories. Before finalizing, I should confirm EL-2.10 actually passes by reviewing the pond utility routing shown on Sheet 15—the vision analysis indicates utilities avoid the pond's main filtration area, with the water line routing west along the retaining wall and wastewater line similarly positioned to avoid direct conflict. Now I need to evaluate EL-2.15, which concerns utility easement boundaries encroaching into proposed public right-of-way. The check requires both utility easements and ROW dedications to be present or proposed. Looking at the site plan, I don't see any ROW dedications shown, and the facts don't indicate a proposed ROW dedication either. The site fronts Valley View Road, which has existing ROW requirements, but without a proposed dedication on this plat, this item wouldn't apply.\n\nHowever, if the site plan shows the existing ROW boundary and any recorded easements cross it, that would trigger the deficiency. The vision results show an existing access and utility easement on the eastern boundary, so I need to verify whether this crosses any ROW line.\n\nThe other easements—the greenbelt access easement in the NE interior and the city drainage easement at the NE corner—likely don't intersect with the ROW. But the 25' drainage easement running across the NE boundary of Lot 1 could potentially cross into it. The site plan does show this easement documented, so that's accounted for.\n\nFor EL-2.15, without the exact overlay of ROW lines and easement boundaries, I can't definitively say whether there's an encroachment. The condition only applies when utility easements and ROW dedications are both proposed or existing. Since no new ROW is being dedicated and the recorded easements don't appear to conflict with existing ROW, this likely doesn't trigger the requirement.\n\nMy final findings: EL-2.11 is not verifiable due to unmarked street tree planting zones and unclear AW routing. EL-2.16 cannot be verified because critical easements aren't shown and I can't confirm licensed items within them. EL-2.22 is not verifiable since transformer pad clearances and AE easement documentation are incomplete. Everything else either doesn't meet applicability criteria or passes based on available information.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":7,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1253,"cache_read_input_tokens":73389,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1253},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b6f63193-ad83-4c2c-8030-773d2a7b74d6","uuid":"b80d8ba3-2fd5-47c3-812a-668c6e60dafc"}
{"level":30,"time":1776954724188,"pid":39427,"hostname":"Williams-MacBook-Pro.local","step":"review-runs","item":"2.md","index":5,"runIndex":"run-2","type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_01NUgRuvawcsP4WTkZHcfsgq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now let me summarize my research findings before moving to the evaluation:\n\n## Research Summary\n\n**Applicability Analysis:**\n\n1. **Regulatory Framework**: The site is located on Valley View Road (Local Street, not CTC/Urban Roadway) in MF-2 zoning, outside the LDC Subchapter E boundary (which runs north/south roughly from Burnet to Hwy 71 to the Loop). The site is NOT in Plaza Saltillo TOD, NOT in NBG district, and NOT in a Hill Country Roadway Corridor. Therefore:\n   - **EL-2.1 through EL-2.8 (CTC/Urban Roadway/TOD/NBG/Hill Country)**: NOT APPLICABLE\n   - **EL-2.24 (Table B parking utilities)**: NOT APPLICABLE (site outside Subchapter E; also under 3-acre threshold)\n\n2. **Detention Pond**: Biofiltration/detention pond proposed in NE corner (Unit 501). Utilities (water, wastewater, electric) visible on Sheet 15 route AROUND the pond (west along retaining wall, south near Unit 501), not under it. **EL-2.10 PASSES**.\n\n3. **Transformer Pads**: Four transformer pads shown on Sheet 21 (Electrical Plan):\n   - Near Bldg. 2, between Bldgs. 4/5, between Bldgs. 6/7, and near Bldg. 8\n   - Only pad near Bldg. 8 has visible \"Vault Easement\" label\n   - No specific clearance dimensions shown (10-ft hot-stick side, 3-ft/5-ft other sides per UCM § 1.10.4)\n   - Three of four pads lack visible AE easement encumbrances\n   - **EL-2.22: NOT-VERIFIABLE** (clearances and AE easements not fully documented)\n\n4. **Critical Missing Easements**: Facts.md documents two recorded easements NOT depicted on current site plan:\n   - **Greenbelt Access & Use Easement (Instrument 2010070731)**: 0.31 acres, NE interior, strict no-build zone\n   - **City of Austin Drainage Easement (Instrument 2011021137)**: 0.130 acres, NE corner, public easement\n   - Together: ~0.44 acres (~19% of NE area) with unlimited City access/control rights\n   - **EL-2.16: NOT-VERIFIABLE** (cannot verify if structures/paving/landscaping proposed within these easements are licensed per UCM § 1.10.10.3 or § 14-11-41)\n\n5. **Street Trees and ROW Planting Zones**: Sheet 31 (Landscape Plan) shows street trees in the ROW street yard area (H-2, H-3 zones) along Valley View Road:\n   - Trees shown with \"SY/M\" labels indicating street yard and mitigation trees\n   - NO root barrier boundaries, planting zone designations, or Utility Gap widths marked\n   - Cannot verify whether Austin Water utilities are routed through or near these zones\n   - Cannot verify compliance with UCM § 2.9.1.C (7-ft setback from tree, 2-ft from utilities, 8-ft Utility Gap)\n   - **EL-2.11: NOT-VERIFIABLE** (street trees present but designated planting zones not marked)\n\n6. **Other Items**: Overhead electric line shown on south boundary (Sheet 31) but EL-2.9 not applicable (overhead utility setback only applies to CTC/Urban Roadway/TOD/NBG frontages, not Local Streets). Utilities shown routed in drive aisles and parking areas but site is outside Subchapter E and under 3-acre Table B threshold.\n\n**NOT FOUND / COULD NOT VERIFY:**\n- Austin Energy coordination letters or AE Design pad sizing documentation\n- Planting zone designations (root barriers, Utility Gap markings) on landscape plan\n- Depicted boundaries for Greenbelt and City of Austin Drainage easements on site plan\n- Specific clearance dimensions for transformer pads\n- Street tree utility exclusion zone markings\n\n---\n\nNow I'll evaluate the checklist items:"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":7,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1253,"cache_read_input_tokens":73389,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1253},"output_tokens":1,"servic